WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:09.510
- Welcome everyone, I
am Priyamvada Natarajan.

00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:13.560
I'm an astrophysicist
and the current director

00:00:13.560 --> 00:00:17.640
of the Franke Program in Science
and the Humanities at Yale,

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:20.640
which is our flagship
interdisciplinary program.

00:00:20.640 --> 00:00:23.910
So I'm delighted to see you
all in the audience today

00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:27.030
gathered for a wonderful
event this evening.

00:00:27.030 --> 00:00:29.550
We have a treat in store, a book talk

00:00:29.550 --> 00:00:33.060
by one of our very own
distinguished faculty members,

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:34.590
Carlos Eire.

00:00:34.590 --> 00:00:37.650
Okay, on to this evening's event,

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:41.940
Carlos Eire is the Lawrason Riggs

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:44.700
Professor of History and Religious Studies

00:00:44.700 --> 00:00:49.020
and he received his PhD from Yale in 1979

00:00:49.020 --> 00:00:53.400
and he specializes in the
social, intellectual, religious

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:56.130
and cultural history of the late medieval

00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:59.970
and early modern Europe with a focus on

00:00:59.970 --> 00:01:03.240
both the Protestant and
Catholic reformations.

00:01:03.240 --> 00:01:07.260
The history of the
supernatural, which is of course

00:01:07.260 --> 00:01:12.180
what really fascinated both
Dr. Kamp who as you all know,

00:01:12.180 --> 00:01:14.040
without whom this event
would not have happened.

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:17.190
We are both fan girls of his work

00:01:17.190 --> 00:01:21.420
on the history of the supernatural
and the history of death.

00:01:21.420 --> 00:01:24.480
Before joining the Yale faculty in 1996,

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:28.350
he taught at St. John's
University in Minnesota,

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:29.970
the University of Virginia,

00:01:29.970 --> 00:01:31.620
and was a member of the Institute

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:34.410
for Advanced Study at
Princeton for two years.

00:01:34.410 --> 00:01:37.650
He's the author of multiple books,

00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:40.747
including "The War Against the Idols,"

00:01:40.747 --> 00:01:43.897
"The Reformation of Worship,
From Erasmus to Calvin,"

00:01:43.897 --> 00:01:48.300
"From Madrid to Purgatory,
the Art and Craft of Dying

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:50.527
in 16th century Spain."

00:01:50.527 --> 00:01:54.570
"A very Brief History of
Eternity," which I love that title,

00:01:54.570 --> 00:01:57.097
I have not read that
book should get that one.

00:01:57.097 --> 00:01:59.107
"Reformations, the Early Modern World,"

00:01:59.107 --> 00:02:02.437
"The Life of St. Theresa
of Avila" biography.

00:02:02.437 --> 00:02:06.450
"And they Flew: A History
of the Impossible,"

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:10.200
which is what we are going
to be hearing about today,

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:11.520
the subject of today's talk.

00:02:11.520 --> 00:02:15.300
He's also the co-author of
"Jews, Christians, Muslims:

00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:18.600
an Introduction to Monotheistic Religions"

00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:23.520
and has also ventured into
later time span in history

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:25.200
to the 20th century

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:27.817
and the Cuban Revolution in the memoir,

00:02:27.817 --> 00:02:32.760
"Waiting for Snow in Havana,"
which was published in 2003

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:36.480
and it won the National
Book Award for nonfiction

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:37.470
in the United States

00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:41.160
and has been translated into
more than a dozen languages.

00:02:41.160 --> 00:02:44.160
His second memoir,
"Learning to Die in Miami,"

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:48.210
explores the exile experience
of being in this country.

00:02:48.210 --> 00:02:51.510
A past president of the Society
for Reformation Research,

00:02:51.510 --> 00:02:54.300
he's currently working on several topics

00:02:54.300 --> 00:02:56.520
in the history of the supernatural.

00:02:56.520 --> 00:02:59.820
His book, "Reformations"
also won many prizes,

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:01.620
including the RR Hawkins Prize

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:03.120
for the best book of the year

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:05.280
from the American Publishers Association.

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:09.540
And the award for the best
book in the humanities in 2017.

00:03:09.540 --> 00:03:12.480
Was also awarded the
Yaroslav Pelikan Prize

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:14.850
by Yale University Press.

00:03:14.850 --> 00:03:19.850
So interestingly, all of his
books are banned in Cuba,

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:21.300
and you know what that means?

00:03:21.300 --> 00:03:25.500
You are immediately a celebrity
once you have a banned book.

00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:28.050
It's almost better than having a stalker,

00:03:28.050 --> 00:03:30.660
which is supposed to be
like the first mark of,

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:32.670
having arrived at celebritydom

00:03:32.670 --> 00:03:36.150
where he has been proclaimed
an enemy of the state.

00:03:36.150 --> 00:03:37.650
That actually sounds scarier

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:39.690
than just having your books banned.

00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:41.220
A distinction that he regards

00:03:41.220 --> 00:03:43.470
as the highest of all his honors.

00:03:43.470 --> 00:03:44.610
So hopefully in the Q&amp;A,

00:03:44.610 --> 00:03:47.460
we can ask him a little
bit more about that.

00:03:47.460 --> 00:03:49.360
So I just want to give you a brief

00:03:50.970 --> 00:03:52.920
notice of upcoming exciting events.

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:55.950
We are hosting a conference
on the nature of inference

00:03:55.950 --> 00:03:57.840
for those of you who've been attending

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:00.480
the Understanding the
Nature of Inference series,

00:04:00.480 --> 00:04:03.150
our conference is on the
14th and 15th of December

00:04:03.150 --> 00:04:05.190
at the Greenberg
Conference Center at Yale.

00:04:05.190 --> 00:04:06.630
And if you would like to attend,

00:04:06.630 --> 00:04:09.210
please go to the website and register.

00:04:09.210 --> 00:04:13.170
And in spring 2024, we have
a ton of exciting events.

00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:15.150
Preview of coming attractions,

00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:18.240
we have Dr. Meredith Broussard

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:20.580
who's investigating the
ways in which big data

00:04:20.580 --> 00:04:22.170
and technology are reinforcing

00:04:22.170 --> 00:04:25.140
race and gender inequality in our society

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:26.310
will be giving us a talk.

00:04:26.310 --> 00:04:28.020
We have a screening of Richland,

00:04:28.020 --> 00:04:30.360
a new documentary on a small town

00:04:30.360 --> 00:04:31.980
struggling with its identity

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:35.280
as a center of weapon
grade plutonium production.

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:38.430
And then we have Christian
Cooper on April 4th,

00:04:38.430 --> 00:04:39.263
he was the victim

00:04:39.263 --> 00:04:40.920
of the Central Park Bird watching incident

00:04:40.920 --> 00:04:43.680
for those of you who may remember.

00:04:43.680 --> 00:04:47.040
So we have a lot of events
coming up in the spring

00:04:47.040 --> 00:04:51.240
and now I want to, without further ado,

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:54.660
invite Carlos to come and speak to us,

00:04:54.660 --> 00:04:56.869
thank you so much for
accepting our invitation.

00:04:56.869 --> 00:04:59.119
(applause)

00:05:01.500 --> 00:05:04.230
- Well, thank you and thanks to everyone

00:05:04.230 --> 00:05:06.210
responsible for making this happen.

00:05:06.210 --> 00:05:09.303
And thank you all for
being here on a cold,

00:05:10.950 --> 00:05:13.113
it's almost December afternoon.

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:20.403
Is a history of the impossible possible?

00:05:23.310 --> 00:05:24.480
History of the impossible

00:05:24.480 --> 00:05:28.773
is the subtitle of my book for a reason.

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:34.760
And this lecture will focus
on this issue of how any

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:44.960
objective historian can
write a history of things

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:48.990
that almost everyone, if not everyone

00:05:48.990 --> 00:05:53.373
thinks cannot happen and
have not ever happened.

00:05:54.330 --> 00:05:59.330
So moving on, how can one write

00:05:59.850 --> 00:06:02.733
a history of the impossible?

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:09.480
I chose two impossible miracles
that most people would agree

00:06:09.480 --> 00:06:11.253
are not only impossible,

00:06:12.090 --> 00:06:16.083
but as an audience member
has already suggested to me,

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:19.740
you're not doing the
history of the impossible,

00:06:19.740 --> 00:06:22.143
you're doing the history
of the ridiculous.

00:06:23.250 --> 00:06:24.910
Or as the review that appeared

00:06:26.760 --> 00:06:30.970
in The Spectator UK website last week

00:06:32.220 --> 00:06:35.973
accused me of sliding into silliness,

00:06:37.530 --> 00:06:41.073
I chose two impossible miracles,

00:06:43.980 --> 00:06:46.010
levitation and bilocation.

00:06:51.930 --> 00:06:54.033
Oh, scary stuff.

00:06:57.900 --> 00:07:01.233
For all scholars, historians
or non historians alike,

00:07:03.690 --> 00:07:05.973
there can be a war between the facts.

00:07:08.880 --> 00:07:13.593
First fact before I get
into Troeltsch and James,

00:07:14.910 --> 00:07:16.530
there is no denying the fact

00:07:16.530 --> 00:07:19.860
that we have testimonies of levitating

00:07:19.860 --> 00:07:22.500
and bilocating human beings,

00:07:22.500 --> 00:07:25.140
that is a fact and it's a
fact that can't be denied,

00:07:25.140 --> 00:07:27.213
we have testimonies.

00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:31.140
But what kind of fact can one deal with

00:07:31.140 --> 00:07:33.213
when one deals with the impossible?

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:38.220
Well, Ernst Troeltsch, social scientist

00:07:38.220 --> 00:07:41.020
came up with a very useful concept

00:07:42.570 --> 00:07:44.010
many, many, many years ago.

00:07:44.010 --> 00:07:48.153
The social fact, what is a social fact?

00:07:49.530 --> 00:07:53.370
Let me explain before I
get to the fine points.

00:07:53.370 --> 00:07:57.810
Social fact is an idea or concept

00:07:57.810 --> 00:08:02.370
or a set of beliefs that are widely held

00:08:02.370 --> 00:08:06.510
and perhaps so widely held
and perhaps even defended

00:08:06.510 --> 00:08:11.400
or demanded, demands
certain kinds of behavior

00:08:11.400 --> 00:08:15.120
that one cannot simply go against them.

00:08:15.120 --> 00:08:17.020
We all live with social facts

00:08:18.150 --> 00:08:20.340
and for instance, in our own day and age,

00:08:20.340 --> 00:08:25.340
it is a social fact that some
words have become threatened,

00:08:25.590 --> 00:08:28.440
there are certain words
you cannot say in public

00:08:28.440 --> 00:08:31.350
unless you like being pilloried.

00:08:31.350 --> 00:08:33.813
That's a social fact, and we live with it.

00:08:35.280 --> 00:08:39.540
Levitation and bilocation,
also social facts

00:08:39.540 --> 00:08:43.263
throughout history and in
some parts of the world.

00:08:44.970 --> 00:08:48.120
And as I used to, when I
used to consult with my

00:08:48.120 --> 00:08:49.860
colleagues who do Asian religions

00:08:49.860 --> 00:08:52.440
and I told them what my project was,

00:08:52.440 --> 00:08:55.050
they were not the least bit impressed

00:08:55.050 --> 00:08:58.230
levitation, bilocation, so what?

00:08:58.230 --> 00:09:01.080
Yeah, of course, of course

00:09:01.080 --> 00:09:05.460
it's a social fact in much
of older Asian religion,

00:09:05.460 --> 00:09:06.513
social fact.

00:09:07.770 --> 00:09:12.183
But there are anomalous events,
let's call them miracles,

00:09:13.020 --> 00:09:16.590
which cannot ever be ascribed
to supernatural causes,

00:09:16.590 --> 00:09:19.323
that's our social fact that we live with.

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:22.080
These anomalous events cannot

00:09:22.080 --> 00:09:25.143
or should not be ascribed
to supernatural causes.

00:09:26.370 --> 00:09:29.730
And it is a social fact
accepted, very widely accepted

00:09:29.730 --> 00:09:33.093
that humans cannot defy
gravity without technology.

00:09:34.050 --> 00:09:36.120
And also that humans cannot be present

00:09:36.120 --> 00:09:39.360
in two places simultaneously.

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:41.640
These are social facts widely accepted

00:09:41.640 --> 00:09:42.843
in our own day and age.

00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:46.263
But then there are also wild facts.

00:09:47.280 --> 00:09:49.380
One of my favorite terms
from William James,

00:09:49.380 --> 00:09:50.733
brother of Henry James,

00:09:54.690 --> 00:09:55.983
what is a wild fact?

00:09:57.270 --> 00:09:58.950
It's an anomalous event

00:09:58.950 --> 00:10:03.950
that just doesn't fit any pigeonhole,

00:10:04.200 --> 00:10:08.160
it's actually like the
proverbial square peg

00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:10.443
that cannot go into the round hole.

00:10:11.790 --> 00:10:13.680
Some anomalous events,

00:10:13.680 --> 00:10:16.563
let's call them wild
facts, defy explanation.

00:10:17.550 --> 00:10:21.570
And these are our wild facts

00:10:21.570 --> 00:10:24.663
when it comes to the two
miracles I've examined.

00:10:25.680 --> 00:10:30.010
We have thousands of reports
of humans hovering and flying

00:10:31.080 --> 00:10:33.390
and we have thousands of reports of humans

00:10:33.390 --> 00:10:36.513
being in two places simultaneously.

00:10:37.530 --> 00:10:41.550
But before I go any
further, the accepted way

00:10:41.550 --> 00:10:45.990
of approaching any of these
wild facts for a historian

00:10:45.990 --> 00:10:50.990
is to place brackets around
the phenomenon itself.

00:10:53.580 --> 00:10:58.580
The event can be studied, the
testimonies can be studied,

00:10:59.010 --> 00:11:02.760
but the proper thing to do is
to take either a functionalist

00:11:02.760 --> 00:11:05.760
or structuralist approach to the event

00:11:05.760 --> 00:11:08.800
and analyze what meaning and purpose

00:11:11.490 --> 00:11:13.650
belief in this event

00:11:13.650 --> 00:11:17.040
has for the people who are testifying

00:11:17.040 --> 00:11:19.050
that they witnessed it.

00:11:19.050 --> 00:11:22.353
So you have to bring your
brackets to the phenomenon.

00:11:23.280 --> 00:11:28.280
I am trying to question the
brackets or their necessity,

00:11:33.420 --> 00:11:37.713
but down to brass tacks,
what is a miracle?

00:11:38.940 --> 00:11:40.170
In the Christian tradition,

00:11:40.170 --> 00:11:44.553
which is the only tradition
I'm focusing on in my book,

00:11:46.500 --> 00:11:49.860
Saint Augustine of Hippo, was challenged

00:11:49.860 --> 00:11:54.600
by a Manechean named
Faustus who argued that

00:11:54.600 --> 00:11:57.840
Christian miracles were contrary to nature

00:11:57.840 --> 00:12:01.653
and therefore false, impossible.

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:05.157
Augustine replied by saying that there is,

00:12:05.157 --> 00:12:08.190
and I quote, "There is
no impropriety in saying

00:12:08.190 --> 00:12:12.930
that God does something against
nature when it is contrary

00:12:12.930 --> 00:12:15.363
to what we know of nature.

00:12:16.350 --> 00:12:17.580
For we give the name nature

00:12:17.580 --> 00:12:20.190
to the usual and known course of nature

00:12:20.190 --> 00:12:22.350
and whatever God does contrary to this

00:12:22.350 --> 00:12:25.530
we call prodigies or miracles."

00:12:25.530 --> 00:12:30.530
So the focus is on whatever
knowledge one has of nature,

00:12:31.020 --> 00:12:33.030
not what the laws of nature are accepted

00:12:33.030 --> 00:12:34.893
in one's day and time might be.

00:12:36.510 --> 00:12:40.120
Aquinas in the 13th century

00:12:42.570 --> 00:12:46.620
had this definition of miracle
in Summa Contra Gentiles,

00:12:46.620 --> 00:12:49.500
a miracle is a sensible
effect produced by God,

00:12:49.500 --> 00:12:53.973
which transcends all the forces of nature,

00:12:55.200 --> 00:12:57.573
with emphasis on transcends.

00:13:00.150 --> 00:13:05.150
And a miracle, Aquinas argued

00:13:05.400 --> 00:13:07.890
does not contradict, destroy, antagonize,

00:13:07.890 --> 00:13:11.040
or violate the forces of nature.

00:13:11.040 --> 00:13:14.730
It is an effect which exceeds
all the inherent powers

00:13:14.730 --> 00:13:16.800
of the forces of nature,

00:13:16.800 --> 00:13:19.110
it is something that
happens as an exception

00:13:19.110 --> 00:13:21.843
to the regular working
of the established order.

00:13:22.830 --> 00:13:26.400
And God, who made the forces of nature

00:13:26.400 --> 00:13:29.310
can cause something to happen at his will,

00:13:29.310 --> 00:13:33.633
which gives evidence of his
dominion over these forces.

00:13:36.810 --> 00:13:41.110
But along comes the
Protestant reformation in 1517

00:13:42.270 --> 00:13:46.533
can be dated exactly
till October 31st, 1517.

00:13:48.540 --> 00:13:50.223
Here's Martin Luther on miracles.

00:13:52.447 --> 00:13:56.190
"Those visible works are
simply signs for the ignorant,

00:13:56.190 --> 00:13:58.500
unbelieving crowd and for their sakes

00:13:58.500 --> 00:14:00.540
that are yet to be attracted."

00:14:00.540 --> 00:14:04.380
But as for us who know
already all we do know

00:14:04.380 --> 00:14:08.427
and believe the gospel,
what do we want them for?"

00:14:09.510 --> 00:14:13.590
Main point, Luther and all the other major

00:14:13.590 --> 00:14:17.730
first and second generation
Protestant reformers

00:14:17.730 --> 00:14:21.660
rejected the existence of any miracles

00:14:21.660 --> 00:14:23.430
post the year 100.

00:14:23.430 --> 00:14:25.980
I'll get to that in a
couple of minutes, why this,

00:14:25.980 --> 00:14:27.333
they thought this was so.

00:14:29.640 --> 00:14:33.900
Second generation reformer,
John Calvin on miracles

00:14:33.900 --> 00:14:35.557
and this is key.

00:14:35.557 --> 00:14:40.557
"We may also fitly remember
that Satan has his miracles,

00:14:40.860 --> 00:14:43.260
which though they are deceitful tricks

00:14:43.260 --> 00:14:47.040
rather than true powers, are
such a sort as to mislead

00:14:47.040 --> 00:14:50.760
the simple-minded and untutored."

00:14:50.760 --> 00:14:52.113
Look at that condescension.

00:14:53.407 --> 00:14:56.010
"Idolatry has been nourished
by wonderful miracles,

00:14:56.010 --> 00:14:57.420
yet these are not sufficient

00:14:57.420 --> 00:15:01.650
to sanction the superstition
either of magicians

00:15:01.650 --> 00:15:06.153
or idolaters," harsh words Calvin.

00:15:07.440 --> 00:15:11.130
Of course, Protestant
reformation was all about

00:15:11.130 --> 00:15:14.550
returning to the Bible, the Bible alone.

00:15:14.550 --> 00:15:17.820
So what about all those
miracles in the Bible?

00:15:17.820 --> 00:15:20.460
In the Hebrew Bible,

00:15:20.460 --> 00:15:22.350
the Old Testament is full of miracles,

00:15:22.350 --> 00:15:24.340
like God crossing the Red Sea

00:15:25.590 --> 00:15:27.723
and many, many, many others.

00:15:28.860 --> 00:15:31.533
And the New Testament
is full of miracles too.

00:15:32.970 --> 00:15:36.420
Is a Christ healing the blind man

00:15:36.420 --> 00:15:38.463
Renaissance period painting.

00:15:39.390 --> 00:15:42.510
But it's not just the gospels
that are full of miracles,

00:15:42.510 --> 00:15:45.630
the acts of the apostles also relates that

00:15:45.630 --> 00:15:46.890
the apostles themselves,

00:15:46.890 --> 00:15:50.160
after Jesus's resurrection and ascension

00:15:50.160 --> 00:15:52.200
performed all sorts of miracles,

00:15:52.200 --> 00:15:54.540
and not just healing miracles

00:15:54.540 --> 00:15:57.090
but wondrous miracles

00:15:57.090 --> 00:16:00.660
such as St. Paul being
bitten by a venomous snake

00:16:00.660 --> 00:16:03.813
and surviving totally unharmed.

00:16:06.090 --> 00:16:07.743
What to do, what to do?

00:16:08.820 --> 00:16:11.070
There are miracles in the Bible,

00:16:11.070 --> 00:16:13.353
why can't there be any miracles now?

00:16:14.850 --> 00:16:18.060
But before I go any further with slides,

00:16:18.060 --> 00:16:22.170
there is this very significant tactical,

00:16:22.170 --> 00:16:26.940
polemical issue that all the
first and second generation

00:16:26.940 --> 00:16:28.983
Protestant reformers had to face.

00:16:30.660 --> 00:16:33.960
Their Catholic opponents
were constantly asking,

00:16:33.960 --> 00:16:37.500
where are your miracles, right?

00:16:37.500 --> 00:16:39.210
We have a long history of miracles

00:16:39.210 --> 00:16:43.290
and we have all these miracles,
where are your miracles?

00:16:43.290 --> 00:16:45.990
Your church must be false.

00:16:45.990 --> 00:16:47.403
So solution,

00:16:51.030 --> 00:16:52.893
miracles stopped at the year 100.

00:16:56.400 --> 00:16:58.200
It's known in Protestant theology

00:16:58.200 --> 00:17:02.103
as the cessation of miracles argument.

00:17:03.450 --> 00:17:07.830
That this doctrine affirms

00:17:07.830 --> 00:17:10.470
that the miracles described
in the Bible ceased

00:17:10.470 --> 00:17:13.983
at the end of the apostolic
age, around the year 100.

00:17:15.870 --> 00:17:17.280
And why 100?

00:17:17.280 --> 00:17:19.590
Well that was the
accepted date more or less

00:17:19.590 --> 00:17:23.010
for the death of the
last of the 12 apostles,

00:17:23.010 --> 00:17:24.093
the apostle John.

00:17:25.110 --> 00:17:27.120
Jesus had commissioned the apostles

00:17:27.120 --> 00:17:31.140
'cause in the gospels
there is blatantly stated

00:17:31.140 --> 00:17:35.583
that he gives them power to do
all these miraculous things.

00:17:37.080 --> 00:17:42.080
But there's nothing else in
the New Testament that says

00:17:42.570 --> 00:17:47.570
that this was, had a
expiration code stamped on it.

00:17:52.770 --> 00:17:55.803
The argument ran like this
for the cessation of miracles.

00:17:58.110 --> 00:18:00.570
Miracles were necessary to convince people

00:18:00.570 --> 00:18:03.423
that Jesus was indeed the son of God.

00:18:05.970 --> 00:18:07.530
Once the first and second

00:18:07.530 --> 00:18:12.530
and perhaps even third generation
of converts were won over

00:18:12.600 --> 00:18:16.083
to belief in Jesus as the
Messiah and the Son of God,

00:18:17.310 --> 00:18:19.300
miracles were no longer necessary

00:18:21.660 --> 00:18:25.920
because the greatest miracle
of all about Christianity

00:18:25.920 --> 00:18:29.370
was the fact that more
people kept converting

00:18:29.370 --> 00:18:31.590
without miracles.

00:18:31.590 --> 00:18:33.693
That was the greatest miracle of all.

00:18:34.800 --> 00:18:38.100
And here's a book from 1917

00:18:38.100 --> 00:18:43.100
by Benjamin Warfield,
"Counterfeit Miracles,"

00:18:43.290 --> 00:18:46.050
which was all about the
cessation of miracles argument

00:18:46.050 --> 00:18:48.720
because in later Protestantism,

00:18:48.720 --> 00:18:52.320
the cessation of miracles
doctrine came under attack.

00:18:52.320 --> 00:18:56.460
Now Warfield here is
defending still in 1917,

00:18:56.460 --> 00:18:58.863
the cessation of miracles argument.

00:18:59.850 --> 00:19:02.103
And there's a long text from John Calvin.

00:19:04.200 --> 00:19:09.200
I won't read all of it, but
this is a important passage.

00:19:09.247 --> 00:19:11.790
"The gift of healing was temporary,

00:19:11.790 --> 00:19:16.587
all are constrained to affirm
and events clearly prove."

00:19:18.150 --> 00:19:19.950
Then he goes on to say anyone,

00:19:19.950 --> 00:19:22.380
the rest of the statement basically says

00:19:22.380 --> 00:19:25.020
that anyone who is claiming a miracle

00:19:25.020 --> 00:19:29.850
past the apostolic age is
aping, that's the verb,

00:19:29.850 --> 00:19:33.723
aping the apostles and
simply fooling people.

00:19:37.350 --> 00:19:38.740
Then came the enlightenment

00:19:41.520 --> 00:19:43.893
following upon the Protestant Reformation.

00:19:44.760 --> 00:19:46.710
And for some reason try this,

00:19:46.710 --> 00:19:51.710
Google the name of any
enlightenment philosopher

00:19:52.050 --> 00:19:56.163
in sunglasses and you're likely
to get an image like this.

00:19:57.960 --> 00:20:02.790
Voltaire, "It is impossible
that a being infinitely wise

00:20:02.790 --> 00:20:05.190
can have made laws to violate them.

00:20:05.190 --> 00:20:07.590
He could not derange the machine

00:20:07.590 --> 00:20:10.080
but with a view of making it work better.

00:20:10.080 --> 00:20:12.960
But it is evident that God
all wise and omnipotent

00:20:12.960 --> 00:20:15.420
originally made this immense machine,

00:20:15.420 --> 00:20:19.323
the universe as good and
perfect as he was able.

00:20:20.190 --> 00:20:22.050
If he saw that some imperfections

00:20:22.050 --> 00:20:24.600
would arise from the nature of matter,

00:20:24.600 --> 00:20:27.270
he provided for that in the beginning,

00:20:27.270 --> 00:20:31.917
and accordingly, he will
never change anything in it."

00:20:32.820 --> 00:20:35.070
How rational, very rational.

00:20:35.070 --> 00:20:38.643
And also an echo of
Faustus, the mannequin.

00:20:43.830 --> 00:20:48.830
Everyone's favorite, David
Hume, dialogue on miracles.

00:20:50.730 --> 00:20:53.193
Miracle is a violation
of the laws of nature,

00:20:54.480 --> 00:20:56.463
Faustus the manechean again.

00:20:57.937 --> 00:20:59.107
"The plain consequence is,

00:20:59.107 --> 00:21:02.400
and it is a general maxim
worthy of our attention

00:21:02.400 --> 00:21:06.540
that no testimony is sufficient
to establish a miracle."

00:21:06.540 --> 00:21:10.200
I'll pause there, we have
all these testimonies, right?

00:21:10.200 --> 00:21:12.330
That's the fact we have,
we have testimonies.

00:21:12.330 --> 00:21:14.190
But he says no testimony

00:21:14.190 --> 00:21:16.500
is sufficient to establish a miracle

00:21:16.500 --> 00:21:20.940
unless the testimony be of
such a kind that its falsehood

00:21:20.940 --> 00:21:23.430
would be more miraculous than the fact

00:21:23.430 --> 00:21:26.853
which it endeavors to
establish, classic Hume.

00:21:27.750 --> 00:21:31.500
A miracle supported by any human testimony

00:21:31.500 --> 00:21:35.553
is more properly a subject
of derision than an argument.

00:21:36.390 --> 00:21:40.440
Hence, how does one do
in a post Humean world,

00:21:40.440 --> 00:21:42.903
a history of the impossible?

00:21:46.650 --> 00:21:50.500
The Catholic church tried to align itself

00:21:51.390 --> 00:21:54.693
against the cessation
of miracles doctrine,

00:21:55.590 --> 00:21:57.540
and against all Protestant arguments

00:21:57.540 --> 00:22:00.300
against Catholic miracles.

00:22:00.300 --> 00:22:04.500
Remember that line from Calvin,
Satan too has his miracles?

00:22:04.500 --> 00:22:07.000
Part of the Protestant
argument was that any

00:22:08.340 --> 00:22:12.730
post apostolic age miracle
claimed by the Catholic church

00:22:13.680 --> 00:22:18.680
could only be a demonic
trick rather than a miracle.

00:22:19.350 --> 00:22:21.870
Yes, Satan has his miracles,

00:22:21.870 --> 00:22:25.953
we'll return to this curious
asymmetry, I call it,

00:22:27.090 --> 00:22:31.530
between Catholic and Protestant
thinking on the miraculous.

00:22:31.530 --> 00:22:34.750
But this man Prospero
Lambertini, an enlightened pope

00:22:37.770 --> 00:22:39.870
who became Pope Benedict the 14th,

00:22:39.870 --> 00:22:43.023
and look at his dates, 1740 to 1758.

00:22:46.020 --> 00:22:48.600
He served, by the way,

00:22:48.600 --> 00:22:53.600
as the devil's advocate
in Rome for several years,

00:22:55.290 --> 00:22:58.540
that's the person charged
in canonization cases with

00:22:59.640 --> 00:23:00.510
calling into doubt

00:23:00.510 --> 00:23:03.540
everything that's being
presented about the same.

00:23:03.540 --> 00:23:07.350
So he knew exactly what was going on.

00:23:07.350 --> 00:23:10.927
There's a wonderful book titled,

00:23:10.927 --> 00:23:15.420
"The Rationalization of
Miracle," by Paolo Parigi,

00:23:15.420 --> 00:23:17.310
if you're interested in this subject,

00:23:17.310 --> 00:23:21.460
he details how it was
that in the 17th century

00:23:22.410 --> 00:23:27.410
the Catholic Church adopted
a more "scientific approach"

00:23:30.480 --> 00:23:34.800
to all these testimonies about
saints and their miracles.

00:23:34.800 --> 00:23:38.523
And actually it was Pope Benedict XIV,

00:23:39.390 --> 00:23:42.570
who more than anyone else,

00:23:42.570 --> 00:23:45.870
stressed that in the canonization process,

00:23:45.870 --> 00:23:47.280
what mattered most

00:23:47.280 --> 00:23:52.023
was heroic virtue rather
than miracle working.

00:23:52.890 --> 00:23:57.890
And it still to this day
remains the primary objective

00:23:57.900 --> 00:24:01.500
in Catholic canonization process.

00:24:01.500 --> 00:24:04.050
Even though you still need miracles,

00:24:04.050 --> 00:24:05.553
they're a secondary nature.

00:24:06.450 --> 00:24:07.923
And he wrote this massive,

00:24:11.640 --> 00:24:16.173
it's a multi-volume book, (speaks Italian)

00:24:18.270 --> 00:24:20.490
on the Beatification
of the Servants of God

00:24:20.490 --> 00:24:23.010
and the Canonization of the Blessing,

00:24:23.010 --> 00:24:25.740
which when I first saw the title, I said,

00:24:25.740 --> 00:24:30.740
oh, I must run and read
this book until I realized

00:24:30.840 --> 00:24:31.807
how humongous it was.

00:24:31.807 --> 00:24:36.453
And that, by the way, this
is still pretty much in use.

00:24:37.380 --> 00:24:39.120
Some tweaking has been done,

00:24:39.120 --> 00:24:42.633
but this is still the
guidebook to this day.

00:24:45.810 --> 00:24:50.810
But okay, Protestant
reformation enlightenment,

00:24:53.400 --> 00:24:55.060
then comes the secular age

00:24:56.610 --> 00:25:00.390
and along comes the
invention of social science,

00:25:00.390 --> 00:25:01.710
and this man in particular

00:25:01.710 --> 00:25:04.473
is a very scary looking man, Max Weber.

00:25:05.940 --> 00:25:10.940
Argued that the key turning
point in western history

00:25:13.110 --> 00:25:18.110
had been this Protestant
cessation of miracles doctrine,

00:25:19.740 --> 00:25:22.143
that is what made the world modern.

00:25:24.120 --> 00:25:28.170
The key point was in English translation,

00:25:28.170 --> 00:25:30.513
the disenchantment of the world,

00:25:32.100 --> 00:25:34.120
in German is much clearer

00:25:37.650 --> 00:25:42.180
the deep magic demagification of the world

00:25:42.180 --> 00:25:45.243
because the German word Zauber is magic.

00:25:46.410 --> 00:25:50.220
So along with the Protestant reformers

00:25:50.220 --> 00:25:52.740
who assigned all miracles to Satan,

00:25:52.740 --> 00:25:55.560
now Weber is pairing up,

00:25:55.560 --> 00:25:57.603
and Protestant reformers did this too.

00:25:58.470 --> 00:26:03.470
Paired Catholic rituals
and miracles with magic,

00:26:06.810 --> 00:26:10.293
which Christians have never
thought as something good.

00:26:11.280 --> 00:26:15.060
So what kind of, Entzauberang is there

00:26:15.060 --> 00:26:17.190
from the Protestant reformers?

00:26:17.190 --> 00:26:20.280
Well, there are no more
sacramental miracles

00:26:20.280 --> 00:26:24.210
like the Eucharist, the bread and wine

00:26:24.210 --> 00:26:29.210
turning into the actual physical
flesh and blood of Christ.

00:26:30.540 --> 00:26:34.080
Although Luther continued to
believe in the real presence,

00:26:34.080 --> 00:26:38.550
he did not believe that
it could be explained.

00:26:38.550 --> 00:26:43.330
And it definitely was
not the body and blood

00:26:44.280 --> 00:26:46.053
under some other guise.

00:26:46.920 --> 00:26:49.290
Other Protestant reformers went further

00:26:49.290 --> 00:26:51.033
denying the real presence.

00:26:52.590 --> 00:26:55.410
All the miracles in the
lives of the saints,

00:26:55.410 --> 00:26:57.420
such as this one depicted here

00:26:57.420 --> 00:26:59.940
by Diego Velazquez's
"Temptation of St. Thomas"

00:26:59.940 --> 00:27:04.080
where angels came to rescue
him from a prostitute

00:27:04.080 --> 00:27:06.090
his family had placed in his room

00:27:06.090 --> 00:27:07.800
so that he would change his mind

00:27:07.800 --> 00:27:10.173
and not join the Dominican order.

00:27:11.430 --> 00:27:15.010
And if COVID does return,
you can get this very nice

00:27:18.270 --> 00:27:22.080
mask, which also, if
you believe in miracles,

00:27:22.080 --> 00:27:26.553
will repel the COVID virus.

00:27:28.620 --> 00:27:33.483
And miracles within mystical
visions, forget about them.

00:27:34.350 --> 00:27:39.350
Human beings do not cross the
boundary into the supernatural

00:27:42.090 --> 00:27:45.120
and cannot, that was
part of the Entzauberung,

00:27:45.120 --> 00:27:48.300
or disenchantment of the world.

00:27:48.300 --> 00:27:50.880
And human beings don't have visions

00:27:50.880 --> 00:27:55.293
of people in heaven like
the Virgin Mary or Jesus.

00:27:56.280 --> 00:27:59.010
No, simply does not happen.

00:27:59.010 --> 00:28:00.303
Mystical ecstasy?

00:28:01.590 --> 00:28:02.423
No.

00:28:04.530 --> 00:28:06.840
And the physical phenomena

00:28:06.840 --> 00:28:09.510
or physical miracles that
accompany mystical ecstasy

00:28:09.510 --> 00:28:12.693
such as the stigmata, no.

00:28:13.890 --> 00:28:15.603
If something like this happens,

00:28:17.340 --> 00:28:20.313
for Protestant theologians, it's demonic,

00:28:21.150 --> 00:28:26.150
for enlightened humans, it's a trick,

00:28:27.360 --> 00:28:28.983
it's probably self-inflicted.

00:28:33.090 --> 00:28:35.553
So here's the tough one, levitation.

00:28:36.990 --> 00:28:38.810
And one reason I picked levitation

00:28:38.810 --> 00:28:42.003
is not only that it's so
scandalously impossible,

00:28:43.020 --> 00:28:46.127
but also that the
individuals that I focus on

00:28:46.127 --> 00:28:49.110
in the 16th and 17th century,

00:28:49.110 --> 00:28:53.640
the testimonies of their
levitation are such that

00:28:53.640 --> 00:28:57.093
if one accepts the
testimonies at face value,

00:28:59.280 --> 00:29:02.410
the levitation could not have been a trick

00:29:04.440 --> 00:29:06.720
perform with wires or ropes

00:29:06.720 --> 00:29:11.720
or any other such contraptions.

00:29:12.000 --> 00:29:13.923
But I'll pause before I forge ahead.

00:29:15.540 --> 00:29:17.523
Levitation was faked all the time,

00:29:18.960 --> 00:29:22.110
and especially in the
16th and 17th century.

00:29:22.110 --> 00:29:25.560
And we have lots of records
of how these fraudsters

00:29:25.560 --> 00:29:30.240
are then processed, caught,
and in many ways, punished.

00:29:30.240 --> 00:29:32.820
And there's a whole section of my book

00:29:32.820 --> 00:29:37.820
that focuses on the frauds
as pretty much a control

00:29:38.250 --> 00:29:39.753
in a scientific experiment.

00:29:40.830 --> 00:29:43.440
All right, you've got someone

00:29:43.440 --> 00:29:48.333
whose levitation are accepted
by the Catholic church.

00:29:50.040 --> 00:29:52.800
For every person whose
levitations are accepted,

00:29:52.800 --> 00:29:55.950
you've got several whose levitations

00:29:55.950 --> 00:29:58.530
have been rejected as fraudulent.

00:29:58.530 --> 00:30:01.360
So there is this back to
the Prospero Lambertini

00:30:02.699 --> 00:30:07.699
and the method of
rationalizing the miraculous

00:30:08.370 --> 00:30:11.673
and approaching miracles empirically.

00:30:14.430 --> 00:30:17.913
Levitation is not found
in the New Testament,

00:30:19.470 --> 00:30:23.070
except for perhaps this cryptic passage

00:30:23.070 --> 00:30:27.187
from St. Paul in Corinthians,
2nd Corinthians quote,

00:30:27.187 --> 00:30:29.670
"I know a man in Christ who 14 years ago,

00:30:29.670 --> 00:30:33.210
was caught up to the third
heaven, whether in the body

00:30:33.210 --> 00:30:36.671
or out of the body, I
don't know, God knows."

00:30:36.671 --> 00:30:39.990
It's the only kind of passage

00:30:39.990 --> 00:30:42.483
that comes close to hinting at levitation.

00:30:43.620 --> 00:30:48.620
But there are some magicians,
non-Christian magicians,

00:30:48.840 --> 00:30:51.690
first, second, third,
fourth, fifth, sixth century

00:30:51.690 --> 00:30:56.690
who are going around
levitating such as Simon Magus.

00:31:00.690 --> 00:31:02.400
But Christian saints levitating,

00:31:02.400 --> 00:31:04.533
you don't find them in the early church.

00:31:07.320 --> 00:31:11.010
You begin to see them in
the later middle ages.

00:31:11.010 --> 00:31:14.740
And the first depiction
of a levitating saint

00:31:16.037 --> 00:31:20.970
that anyone knows has been painted

00:31:20.970 --> 00:31:22.390
is this one by Giotto

00:31:23.310 --> 00:31:26.613
of the Ecstasy of St. Francis, 1295.

00:31:28.380 --> 00:31:30.840
And in the life of Francis by Bonaventure,

00:31:30.840 --> 00:31:34.687
here's the text that goes with this image.

00:31:34.687 --> 00:31:36.930
"There he was beheld praying by night,

00:31:36.930 --> 00:31:39.990
his hands stretched out
after the manner of a cross,

00:31:39.990 --> 00:31:42.810
his whole body uplifted from the earth

00:31:42.810 --> 00:31:45.570
and wrapped in a shining cloud

00:31:45.570 --> 00:31:49.260
as though the wondrous
illumination of the body

00:31:49.260 --> 00:31:53.427
were a witness unto the wondrous
enlightenment of his mind."

00:31:55.110 --> 00:31:58.623
So he's not only levitating, he's glowing.

00:32:03.810 --> 00:32:04.643
Let's skip ahead.

00:32:04.643 --> 00:32:07.380
There are numerous levitating saints

00:32:07.380 --> 00:32:08.820
in the later Middle ages.

00:32:08.820 --> 00:32:11.130
As a matter of fact, with
each passing century,

00:32:11.130 --> 00:32:14.853
the numbers of levitating
saints gets higher and higher.

00:32:16.260 --> 00:32:18.963
And we get to the 16th century.

00:32:20.310 --> 00:32:23.640
It's a response to the
Protestant reformation.

00:32:23.640 --> 00:32:27.543
Okay, you guys don't
have miracles, but we do.

00:32:29.190 --> 00:32:33.240
And the number of
levitating saints increases

00:32:33.240 --> 00:32:36.033
in the 16th century and 17th century.

00:32:37.530 --> 00:32:40.680
But part of this increase,
it has a lot to do

00:32:40.680 --> 00:32:43.140
with the invention of the printing press

00:32:43.140 --> 00:32:45.600
and the publication of hagiographies

00:32:45.600 --> 00:32:47.010
or lives of the saints,

00:32:47.010 --> 00:32:48.570
this is one reason it's much easier

00:32:48.570 --> 00:32:51.010
to find levitating saints

00:32:52.710 --> 00:32:56.010
in the early modern period
than even the later centuries

00:32:56.010 --> 00:33:00.690
of the Middle Ages, is that
you've got hagiographies

00:33:00.690 --> 00:33:03.393
that are printed and distributed widely.

00:33:04.770 --> 00:33:08.010
And here we have the
founder of the Jesuit order,

00:33:08.010 --> 00:33:10.720
St. Ignatius Loyola, who was observed

00:33:11.850 --> 00:33:13.263
levitating and glowing.

00:33:14.280 --> 00:33:16.410
He was observed many times at night,

00:33:16.410 --> 00:33:17.970
his room filled with a bright light,

00:33:17.970 --> 00:33:20.640
he's seen raised up in the
air with his knees bent,

00:33:20.640 --> 00:33:22.567
weeping, sighing, and saying,

00:33:22.567 --> 00:33:25.107
"My God, how infinitely good you are."

00:33:26.520 --> 00:33:31.520
And the Jesuit hagiographers
liked this scene very much.

00:33:33.750 --> 00:33:37.440
These three images are
from graphic hagiographies,

00:33:37.440 --> 00:33:41.580
a genre of hagiography
that became very popular

00:33:41.580 --> 00:33:46.200
from the mid 16th century
into the 17th century.

00:33:46.200 --> 00:33:48.210
Where you know, you pick
up the life of the saint

00:33:48.210 --> 00:33:50.253
and it's basically like a comic book.

00:33:51.900 --> 00:33:55.080
And the text is, and I left
the text here so you could see

00:33:55.080 --> 00:33:58.500
how little text there is
accompanying the image.

00:33:58.500 --> 00:34:01.290
So image after image after image.

00:34:01.290 --> 00:34:05.010
And notice also in these depictions,

00:34:05.010 --> 00:34:07.293
there is always a witness,

00:34:08.730 --> 00:34:11.733
the testimony is linked to the image.

00:34:15.420 --> 00:34:20.420
St Teresa of Avila also
levitating and glowing.

00:34:22.320 --> 00:34:26.010
And this happened in front
of the Bishop of Avila

00:34:26.010 --> 00:34:28.233
and a crowd of witnesses.

00:34:30.360 --> 00:34:32.310
I'll pause for a second and add something

00:34:32.310 --> 00:34:35.700
about St. Teresa that's not in the slides.

00:34:35.700 --> 00:34:40.700
St. Teresa hated to levitate
and tried to resist it

00:34:42.360 --> 00:34:43.920
and begged her nuns,

00:34:43.920 --> 00:34:46.500
actually ordered them because
she was their superior.

00:34:46.500 --> 00:34:49.143
If you see me going up, hold me down.

00:34:50.130 --> 00:34:52.563
If I do go up, pull me down.

00:34:53.520 --> 00:34:56.310
And she would hold on to
any object that she could

00:34:56.310 --> 00:34:58.260
as she felt it coming on

00:34:58.260 --> 00:35:02.100
and tried to stay
grounded, unsuccessfully.

00:35:02.100 --> 00:35:07.100
Including one time in the
kitchen while she was frying eggs

00:35:07.140 --> 00:35:11.190
with the last few drops,
precious drops of olive oil

00:35:11.190 --> 00:35:13.620
that her convent had left.

00:35:13.620 --> 00:35:16.890
And she goes up with a
frying pan in her hand.

00:35:16.890 --> 00:35:18.510
But the nuns are under obedience,

00:35:18.510 --> 00:35:19.980
so they're trying to pull her down,

00:35:19.980 --> 00:35:23.343
but they're worried that
precious oil is going to spill,

00:35:24.330 --> 00:35:26.460
but they cannot pull her down.

00:35:26.460 --> 00:35:27.570
I'll add this too,

00:35:27.570 --> 00:35:29.880
this is very common of early modern

00:35:29.880 --> 00:35:32.463
as well as medieval levitations.

00:35:33.840 --> 00:35:34.840
When does it happen?

00:35:35.940 --> 00:35:38.850
It happens during mystical ecstasy.

00:35:38.850 --> 00:35:42.693
It is a byproduct of mystical ecstasy.

00:35:43.740 --> 00:35:45.840
And it is not something that is willed,

00:35:45.840 --> 00:35:48.660
it's against your will.

00:35:48.660 --> 00:35:53.160
But more than that, you see it here.

00:35:53.160 --> 00:35:56.943
You see it in these two and other images.

00:35:58.530 --> 00:36:02.640
In mystical ecstasy, certain
kinds of mystical ecstasy,

00:36:02.640 --> 00:36:05.940
the body goes into a cataleptic seizure

00:36:05.940 --> 00:36:09.873
and that's a medical term, becomes rigid.

00:36:11.100 --> 00:36:13.420
The senses cease to work

00:36:14.880 --> 00:36:16.110
whatever position you're in

00:36:16.110 --> 00:36:19.053
when you enter mystical
ecstasy, you're frozen.

00:36:20.670 --> 00:36:24.573
And it's kind of humorous,
but also very sad.

00:36:26.340 --> 00:36:30.840
Many of these levitating
saints, their communities,

00:36:30.840 --> 00:36:32.253
prick them with needles,

00:36:33.570 --> 00:36:37.173
place candle lit candles
right in front of their eyes.

00:36:38.250 --> 00:36:42.360
Or my favorite, blow on them

00:36:42.360 --> 00:36:45.240
because they're so nearly weightless,

00:36:45.240 --> 00:36:49.390
blow on them to watch their
body waft across the room

00:36:51.240 --> 00:36:53.943
while they are in this cataleptic state.

00:36:56.610 --> 00:37:01.380
So Teresa resisted, and
we don't have time for it,

00:37:01.380 --> 00:37:04.830
but she is the, not the only one,

00:37:04.830 --> 00:37:07.440
but the one levitator

00:37:07.440 --> 00:37:10.950
who has devoted the most page space

00:37:10.950 --> 00:37:14.700
in her writings to describing

00:37:14.700 --> 00:37:18.690
what it feels like to levitate
and what it feels like

00:37:18.690 --> 00:37:21.613
to resist these levitations.

00:37:23.820 --> 00:37:26.460
And there she is levitating

00:37:26.460 --> 00:37:28.480
jointly with St. John of the cross

00:37:29.520 --> 00:37:33.450
one Trinity Sunday in either 1573 or '74,

00:37:33.450 --> 00:37:35.340
nobody has fixed the date,

00:37:35.340 --> 00:37:37.620
but they were discussing the trinity

00:37:37.620 --> 00:37:41.583
and all of a sudden
whoop, they both go up.

00:37:43.140 --> 00:37:47.193
And here's another depiction of this.

00:37:48.390 --> 00:37:53.390
I began this project 40
years ago in June of 1983

00:37:57.150 --> 00:38:00.483
when visiting the convent
of the incarnation in Avila.

00:38:02.010 --> 00:38:03.933
Tour guide is taking us around.

00:38:06.060 --> 00:38:09.030
Here's the kitchen where Teresa cooked

00:38:09.030 --> 00:38:11.370
with her pots and pans.

00:38:11.370 --> 00:38:13.473
Here's the refectory where the nuns ate.

00:38:14.640 --> 00:38:17.790
And one of my favorites,
here's the staircase

00:38:17.790 --> 00:38:21.240
where the devil pushed
Teresa down the stairs

00:38:21.240 --> 00:38:22.353
and she broke her arm.

00:38:23.190 --> 00:38:26.100
And then we got to this
spot, the locutorio,

00:38:26.100 --> 00:38:28.533
where the nuns spoke
with the outside world.

00:38:29.910 --> 00:38:32.490
And here's the spot where
Teresa and John of the cross

00:38:32.490 --> 00:38:34.640
levitated together for
the very first time,

00:38:37.290 --> 00:38:38.493
my head exploded.

00:38:39.420 --> 00:38:41.080
It was a cognitive

00:38:43.650 --> 00:38:46.440
experience like I have never had before.

00:38:46.440 --> 00:38:48.540
There's nothing emotional about it,

00:38:48.540 --> 00:38:51.440
it's purely rational, Kant
would've been very proud of me.

00:38:53.370 --> 00:38:58.370
Here's it's a fact to
the people in this place,

00:38:58.530 --> 00:39:00.270
this is a fact.

00:39:00.270 --> 00:39:04.650
And from here on in, on and
off, on and off, over 40 years

00:39:04.650 --> 00:39:05.883
until COVID hit.

00:39:07.230 --> 00:39:10.900
And then this book was
written mostly during COVID

00:39:11.940 --> 00:39:16.053
and by then a miracle,

00:39:17.070 --> 00:39:19.680
the older, a text was, a printed text,

00:39:19.680 --> 00:39:22.960
the older it was, more likely
it was, I would find it online

00:39:23.970 --> 00:39:26.280
and every hagiography I needed,

00:39:26.280 --> 00:39:31.280
every single one except two
or three was online for me.

00:39:32.970 --> 00:39:35.250
So I didn't need to leave the house.

00:39:35.250 --> 00:39:39.600
And from the bowels of
Yale library system,

00:39:39.600 --> 00:39:41.400
if I requested a book,

00:39:41.400 --> 00:39:44.430
it would be neatly wrapped in brown paper

00:39:44.430 --> 00:39:49.353
and mailed to my
doorstep, another miracle.

00:39:52.740 --> 00:39:55.653
So greatest levitator of all time.

00:39:56.940 --> 00:39:59.913
St. Joseph of Cupertino, 1663,

00:40:01.200 --> 00:40:03.750
I mean 1603 to 1663.

00:40:03.750 --> 00:40:06.240
And you have here three
different depictions

00:40:06.240 --> 00:40:08.073
of the same levitation.

00:40:10.080 --> 00:40:14.310
He looks more like Superman
than any other character

00:40:14.310 --> 00:40:16.620
you might be familiar with.

00:40:16.620 --> 00:40:18.783
What's so special about St. Joseph?

00:40:22.020 --> 00:40:23.130
And look at the time period,

00:40:23.130 --> 00:40:28.130
1603-1663 Protestant Reformation
is already triumphed,

00:40:30.120 --> 00:40:31.953
taken over great chunks of Europe.

00:40:33.300 --> 00:40:38.040
So this is a century
after Luther and Calvin.

00:40:38.040 --> 00:40:41.040
But from very early
age, he was very pious,

00:40:41.040 --> 00:40:43.773
he would go into mystical
ecstasies and levitate.

00:40:45.420 --> 00:40:49.473
But he also happened to
be mentally challenged.

00:40:50.310 --> 00:40:53.640
And his name, his nickname
given to him by locals

00:40:53.640 --> 00:40:56.763
was Boccaperta, Open mouth.

00:40:58.710 --> 00:41:01.960
So the village idiot basically

00:41:03.480 --> 00:41:07.740
who was rejected twice by,
first by the Franciscans

00:41:07.740 --> 00:41:09.540
and secondly the Capuchins

00:41:09.540 --> 00:41:13.953
because he couldn't do
anything right except levitate,

00:41:16.260 --> 00:41:17.553
which hindered his work.

00:41:20.550 --> 00:41:22.750
Long story short, he ends up becoming

00:41:23.640 --> 00:41:25.230
member of the Franciscan order

00:41:25.230 --> 00:41:26.793
and being ordained a priest,

00:41:29.430 --> 00:41:33.810
his ordination was meant to be
a humiliation to his superior

00:41:33.810 --> 00:41:38.810
who was treating him like,
pardon the expression, a moron.

00:41:38.940 --> 00:41:41.430
Oh, so you're treating this man this way,

00:41:41.430 --> 00:41:43.560
I'll teach you some humility.

00:41:43.560 --> 00:41:44.910
You prepare him to be a priest

00:41:44.910 --> 00:41:46.660
and you make sure he gets ordained.

00:41:48.090 --> 00:41:50.160
He managed to pass the test

00:41:50.160 --> 00:41:53.910
'cause the only question
asked of him was on the only

00:41:53.910 --> 00:41:57.543
passage out of the entire
Bible that he knew by memory,

00:41:58.560 --> 00:42:01.713
which is why he is the
patron saint of test takers.

00:42:04.890 --> 00:42:09.603
Processed by the inquisition, he passes.

00:42:11.280 --> 00:42:14.070
Why would the inquisition processes?

00:42:14.070 --> 00:42:18.030
Well this could be demonic you know,

00:42:18.030 --> 00:42:19.560
same thing with St. Teresa,

00:42:19.560 --> 00:42:22.500
same thing with all these
other early modern levitators,

00:42:22.500 --> 00:42:25.560
they had to clear away the suspicion

00:42:25.560 --> 00:42:27.840
that this might be the devil at work.

00:42:27.840 --> 00:42:31.323
And what was the best way
to clear away the suspicion?

00:42:32.940 --> 00:42:36.600
Humility, humility, these
people are not trying

00:42:36.600 --> 00:42:39.390
to attract attention to themselves,

00:42:39.390 --> 00:42:42.153
they're always humble in
their dealings with others.

00:42:47.010 --> 00:42:51.240
Joseph not only levitated, he flew

00:42:51.240 --> 00:42:56.043
and he flew forwards and
backwards, sometimes upside down.

00:42:57.420 --> 00:42:59.370
And here's an image of him

00:42:59.370 --> 00:43:03.360
upon seeing the shrine of
the holy house of Loreto,

00:43:03.360 --> 00:43:04.383
he goes up.

00:43:05.760 --> 00:43:08.100
And many descriptions of him

00:43:08.100 --> 00:43:11.250
are going up as high as trees treetops,

00:43:11.250 --> 00:43:15.660
one time he goes up to
a treetop levitating

00:43:15.660 --> 00:43:17.820
and then comes out of his
ecstasy in the treetop

00:43:17.820 --> 00:43:21.213
and they have to get a very
tall ladder to help him down.

00:43:23.040 --> 00:43:26.730
And this painter is unknown,

00:43:26.730 --> 00:43:29.850
this just sold at auction last year.

00:43:29.850 --> 00:43:33.030
No price disclosed, no owner disclosed,

00:43:33.030 --> 00:43:36.963
I'd like to know who has
this hanging in their palace.

00:43:39.720 --> 00:43:43.140
Joseph had triggers, what
triggered his ecstasies?

00:43:43.140 --> 00:43:48.140
All sorts of things, religious
imagery especially hence,

00:43:49.140 --> 00:43:51.693
his flying to that cross.

00:43:53.730 --> 00:43:54.840
Sacred places,

00:43:54.840 --> 00:43:58.923
seeing the shrine of the
Holy House of Loreto.

00:44:00.510 --> 00:44:05.010
And the image to the left
is actually St. Joseph

00:44:05.010 --> 00:44:07.380
flying over the heads

00:44:07.380 --> 00:44:11.280
of the Spanish Viceroy
in Italy and his wife

00:44:11.280 --> 00:44:14.670
and their entire entourage
and his wife fainted

00:44:14.670 --> 00:44:17.460
and had to be revived with smelling salts,

00:44:17.460 --> 00:44:22.460
you see her there passed
out right under Joseph.

00:44:22.890 --> 00:44:26.040
But the reason he flew over
their heads is he had seen

00:44:26.040 --> 00:44:27.660
this image of the Virgin Mary

00:44:27.660 --> 00:44:29.880
and was just immediately, ah, mama,

00:44:29.880 --> 00:44:32.253
that's what he called these images, mama.

00:44:36.390 --> 00:44:40.290
Other triggers, the
sacraments saying Mass.

00:44:40.290 --> 00:44:43.830
Most of his levitations
occurred while saying Mass.

00:44:43.830 --> 00:44:46.833
And I'll return to this
in a minute or two.

00:44:47.700 --> 00:44:51.333
And people in need of
healing or of conversion.

00:44:52.710 --> 00:44:56.640
These two paintings
blend all these triggers.

00:44:56.640 --> 00:45:00.810
On the left, you have him
dragging up a young man

00:45:00.810 --> 00:45:04.350
who had lost his mind and
was considered insane.

00:45:04.350 --> 00:45:08.313
He placed his hand on him to
heal him and went up with him.

00:45:10.260 --> 00:45:14.250
And it's a double miracle,
a levitation and a healing.

00:45:14.250 --> 00:45:17.040
The other image is St. Joseph of Cupertino

00:45:17.040 --> 00:45:19.260
saying mass levitating at mass,

00:45:19.260 --> 00:45:24.260
and the person astounded
is Johan Friedrich,

00:45:24.900 --> 00:45:29.900
the Lutheran Duke of Saxony
who converted to Catholicism

00:45:31.020 --> 00:45:33.963
after seeing Joseph
levitate in this manner.

00:45:35.580 --> 00:45:40.263
And again, I have some
pauses, this is another pause.

00:45:42.683 --> 00:45:46.780
A scientist in the UK, in Wales

00:45:48.000 --> 00:45:50.490
got an advanced copy of
my book and emailed me,

00:45:50.490 --> 00:45:51.460
he was very excited

00:45:54.270 --> 00:45:58.483
by my descriptions of
St. Joseph's levitations.

00:45:59.340 --> 00:46:01.713
And the email said
basically, I'm paraphrasing,

00:46:03.780 --> 00:46:08.780
I am so happy this proves
so much about my theory

00:46:08.970 --> 00:46:12.963
of levitations being
a break in time space.

00:46:16.140 --> 00:46:21.090
And what he likes so much
about Joseph is the fact that,

00:46:21.090 --> 00:46:25.420
this cataleptic posture
involved not only his body

00:46:27.630 --> 00:46:31.350
but his clothing, which
retained its folds,

00:46:31.350 --> 00:46:33.033
the folds would not change.

00:46:34.170 --> 00:46:36.150
And even when upside down,

00:46:36.150 --> 00:46:39.210
the habit would not come
flopping down over his head,

00:46:39.210 --> 00:46:40.113
it would stay.

00:46:41.370 --> 00:46:45.420
And when coming out of
the cataleptic seizure,

00:46:45.420 --> 00:46:47.820
especially when saying mass,

00:46:47.820 --> 00:46:51.900
Joseph, even after three
hours, would pick up

00:46:51.900 --> 00:46:56.900
whatever spot in the missile
he was at, the next word.

00:46:58.200 --> 00:47:02.310
So this scientist got
all excited because yes,

00:47:02.310 --> 00:47:06.510
he's outside of time
space, there's no time.

00:47:06.510 --> 00:47:08.430
Oh, he was so excited.

00:47:08.430 --> 00:47:11.130
I couldn't be as excited
'cause I couldn't understand

00:47:11.130 --> 00:47:14.433
the technical terminology
he was throwing at me.

00:47:17.070 --> 00:47:21.690
He went into ecstasy in
solitude as well as in public.

00:47:21.690 --> 00:47:25.860
And even went over the
head of Pope Urban VIII,

00:47:25.860 --> 00:47:30.750
who supposedly exclaimed, "If
this man dies before I do,

00:47:30.750 --> 00:47:34.260
I will testify at his canonization inquest

00:47:34.260 --> 00:47:36.090
that he flew over my head."

00:47:36.090 --> 00:47:37.923
So talk about testimonies.

00:47:38.880 --> 00:47:43.880
This is one of the highest placed people

00:47:44.070 --> 00:47:48.273
who testified that he
did in fact defy gravity.

00:47:49.620 --> 00:47:54.060
So in the baroque period,
17th century mostly,

00:47:54.060 --> 00:47:57.000
you begin to see in Catholic sacred art,

00:47:57.000 --> 00:48:00.911
more and more depictions
of levitating saints

00:48:00.911 --> 00:48:04.620
and especially of
medieval levitating saints

00:48:04.620 --> 00:48:08.433
who had not received attention
from medieval artists.

00:48:09.480 --> 00:48:12.960
So here you have a
very, very odd rendition

00:48:12.960 --> 00:48:17.220
of the stigmatization of St.
Francis by Vicente Carducho,

00:48:17.220 --> 00:48:20.250
an Italian artist who
hispanicized his name

00:48:20.250 --> 00:48:25.170
from Carduchi to Carducho, who
his father is also an artist

00:48:25.170 --> 00:48:27.020
who was brought to the Spanish court.

00:48:28.170 --> 00:48:31.170
And on the right you also
have another Carducho,

00:48:31.170 --> 00:48:33.660
levitation of St. Anthony of Padua,

00:48:33.660 --> 00:48:36.243
a contemporary of St. Francis.

00:48:40.290 --> 00:48:43.960
And a levitation of St.
Dominic, 13th century

00:48:45.300 --> 00:48:49.110
and a another Carducho vision of St. Hugo,

00:48:49.110 --> 00:48:51.573
one of the early carthusian monks.

00:48:52.440 --> 00:48:54.750
Carducho was a specialist from what,

00:48:54.750 --> 00:48:57.570
as far as I can tell,
I'm not an art historian.

00:48:57.570 --> 00:48:59.040
And every colleague I've ever had

00:48:59.040 --> 00:49:01.200
in any art history
department always reminds me,

00:49:01.200 --> 00:49:03.513
you are not an art historian.

00:49:05.580 --> 00:49:09.220
But Carducho has more
paintings representing

00:49:10.620 --> 00:49:13.263
levitating saints than
anyone else I found.

00:49:15.030 --> 00:49:16.710
So the past, the history,

00:49:16.710 --> 00:49:21.630
the miracle is therefore
thrown to the past for display,

00:49:21.630 --> 00:49:25.653
either on monastery or
convent walls or church walls.

00:49:27.840 --> 00:49:31.050
And here's one of the
most amazing depictions,

00:49:31.050 --> 00:49:32.890
baroque depictions of levitation

00:49:34.230 --> 00:49:37.140
from Murillo and other Spanish artists.

00:49:37.140 --> 00:49:39.060
There are arguments still going on

00:49:39.060 --> 00:49:41.010
as to who this Franciscan friar is,

00:49:41.010 --> 00:49:43.863
doesn't matter, he was in the kitchen,

00:49:44.940 --> 00:49:46.890
he, under his bow of obedience,

00:49:46.890 --> 00:49:50.850
he was supposed to do kitchen
work, but he went into ecstasy

00:49:50.850 --> 00:49:53.160
and started levitating and glowing.

00:49:53.160 --> 00:49:56.670
So angels came to do his work.

00:49:56.670 --> 00:49:57.780
And there on the left,

00:49:57.780 --> 00:50:00.930
you see the eyewitnesses
who come and find him.

00:50:00.930 --> 00:50:05.100
This painting is huge and it
hangs in the Louvre Museum.

00:50:05.100 --> 00:50:10.100
It was stolen by Napoleon's
army and never returned.

00:50:11.220 --> 00:50:14.433
So add this to the list
with the Elgin marbles,

00:50:15.390 --> 00:50:17.253
things that need to be returned.

00:50:20.070 --> 00:50:21.813
The other miracle is bilocation.

00:50:22.800 --> 00:50:27.800
And here's your familiar
figure, ah, here's where we are,

00:50:27.870 --> 00:50:29.853
and there it is.

00:50:30.840 --> 00:50:33.810
You can buy that inflatable
scream figure, by the way,

00:50:33.810 --> 00:50:34.893
just Google it.

00:50:36.630 --> 00:50:37.620
Don't tell my wife,

00:50:37.620 --> 00:50:39.770
she's getting that for
a Christmas present.

00:50:44.460 --> 00:50:47.520
Giotto, also bilocation by St. Francis.

00:50:47.520 --> 00:50:50.103
So again, throwing
things back to the past.

00:50:51.960 --> 00:50:54.990
Greatest bilocator, not the greatest

00:50:54.990 --> 00:50:59.370
'cause I found one later,
but an extreme bilocator.

00:50:59.370 --> 00:51:03.990
So Maria De Agreda, almost
the same exact dates

00:51:03.990 --> 00:51:08.990
as St. Joseph, 1602 to 1665,

00:51:09.000 --> 00:51:13.750
a Franciscan nun who bilocated repeatedly

00:51:15.390 --> 00:51:20.163
and crossed the ocean,

00:51:21.090 --> 00:51:23.370
never left Agreda physically,

00:51:23.370 --> 00:51:28.020
but was seen by the Jumano natives

00:51:28.020 --> 00:51:30.720
in present day New Mexico and Texas,

00:51:30.720 --> 00:51:35.343
visited them reportedly over
500 times to Christianize them.

00:51:36.180 --> 00:51:39.090
This is a tricky case, very tricky.

00:51:39.090 --> 00:51:42.720
But she acquired such a reputation

00:51:42.720 --> 00:51:47.720
as being a genuine bilocator
that she is a legend

00:51:48.180 --> 00:51:49.893
in the American southwest.

00:51:50.820 --> 00:51:53.250
And by sheer coincidence, one of my

00:51:53.250 --> 00:51:55.410
teaching fellows last year

00:51:55.410 --> 00:51:58.743
was traveling through San Angelo, Texas.

00:51:59.580 --> 00:52:04.090
Sent me a photo, San Angelo,
Texas has erected a huge statue

00:52:04.980 --> 00:52:09.000
of the lady in blue as
she was known in that area

00:52:09.000 --> 00:52:10.890
'cause her habit had a little bit of blue.

00:52:10.890 --> 00:52:14.100
So she crossed the ocean back and forth.

00:52:14.100 --> 00:52:18.300
And as if that were not enough,
another impossible miracle,

00:52:18.300 --> 00:52:23.190
I decided to not focus
on too much in the book.

00:52:23.190 --> 00:52:25.510
The Virgin Mary appeared to her constantly

00:52:27.270 --> 00:52:30.663
and dictated her
autobiography to this nun.

00:52:32.100 --> 00:52:33.900
And she wrote it down,

00:52:33.900 --> 00:52:36.813
was ordered to burn it, she burned it.

00:52:37.680 --> 00:52:39.150
Another superior came in and says,

00:52:39.150 --> 00:52:40.800
where's that life of Mary?

00:52:40.800 --> 00:52:44.253
Oh, I was asked to burn it,
oh, write it over again.

00:52:45.300 --> 00:52:47.887
It's over a million words long.

00:52:47.887 --> 00:52:50.853
"The Mystical City of
God" is the English title.

00:52:52.290 --> 00:52:55.623
And it guess which film
relied on it heavily?

00:52:57.780 --> 00:53:00.213
Mel Gibson's the passion of the Christ.

00:53:04.170 --> 00:53:06.780
She describes things that
are not in the New Testament.

00:53:06.780 --> 00:53:10.690
So it's an even iffier
sort of impossible miracle

00:53:12.720 --> 00:53:15.340
because her mystical city of God

00:53:16.560 --> 00:53:18.993
could be considered a fifth Gospel.

00:53:20.010 --> 00:53:22.770
And there in this painting,
you see her paired up

00:53:22.770 --> 00:53:27.540
with none other than John the evangelist,

00:53:27.540 --> 00:53:29.940
who was also for a long time

00:53:29.940 --> 00:53:32.100
believed to be the author
of the Book of Revelation

00:53:32.100 --> 00:53:35.583
or the Apocalypse with the new
Jerusalem in the background.

00:53:38.700 --> 00:53:42.243
And there you see the sheer
size of this work of hers,

00:53:43.980 --> 00:53:45.420
which ended up being condemned

00:53:45.420 --> 00:53:49.083
by the faculty theological
faculty of the Sorbone,

00:53:51.030 --> 00:53:55.470
but approved by no surprise,

00:53:55.470 --> 00:54:00.470
Salamanca, Alcala, Luvent,
which was under Spanish control,

00:54:00.690 --> 00:54:04.413
but inexplicable, Toulouse,
they all approved it.

00:54:10.440 --> 00:54:12.970
Here's a fact nobody disputes

00:54:14.100 --> 00:54:18.010
the first man to ever levitate

00:54:20.400 --> 00:54:23.820
with help in a hot air balloon,

00:54:23.820 --> 00:54:28.043
Jean-Francois Pilatre de
Rozier, love that name, 1783.

00:54:34.470 --> 00:54:38.583
But then, to wrap up, there is
the wrong kind of levitation,

00:54:40.740 --> 00:54:43.233
witches were believed to levitate and fly.

00:54:44.340 --> 00:54:48.570
And just read the Malleus
Maleficarum from the 15th century,

00:54:48.570 --> 00:54:51.910
the witchcraft treatise of
all of witchcraft treatises

00:54:52.860 --> 00:54:57.360
tells you how they fly to
their sabbaths, how they,

00:54:57.360 --> 00:55:00.990
rub oil on themselves and fly.

00:55:00.990 --> 00:55:03.660
Protestants continued to believe

00:55:03.660 --> 00:55:05.370
just about a hundred percent

00:55:05.370 --> 00:55:08.853
of Catholic medieval Catholic demonology.

00:55:10.440 --> 00:55:13.650
But they rejected much of the theology

00:55:13.650 --> 00:55:14.880
and all of the miracles,

00:55:14.880 --> 00:55:18.723
that's the asymmetry I
was referring to before.

00:55:20.370 --> 00:55:25.370
So the peak period for flying saints

00:55:26.640 --> 00:55:30.630
is also the peak period for
flying witches and witch hunts,

00:55:30.630 --> 00:55:33.180
which is why I say the
16th and 17th century

00:55:33.180 --> 00:55:36.120
is the peak period for flying humans

00:55:36.120 --> 00:55:39.723
before the invention of hot
air balloons and airplanes.

00:55:45.960 --> 00:55:49.590
Levitation was possible for Protestants,

00:55:49.590 --> 00:55:54.420
the image on the left or flying witches

00:55:54.420 --> 00:55:56.763
and the devil from an English text.

00:55:57.900 --> 00:56:01.530
On the right, you see how long this idea

00:56:01.530 --> 00:56:04.050
or belief might have persisted in Spain,

00:56:04.050 --> 00:56:07.740
here's a painting by Francisco
Goya, right on the edge

00:56:07.740 --> 00:56:10.590
between the 18th and 19th century.

00:56:10.590 --> 00:56:13.170
A series of paintings on witchcraft

00:56:13.170 --> 00:56:17.373
commissioned by some patron
I don't know much about.

00:56:19.620 --> 00:56:22.290
And to show how perverse
historians can be,

00:56:22.290 --> 00:56:24.003
or at least how perverse I am.

00:56:25.380 --> 00:56:29.070
One of the happiest days
in my research of this book

00:56:29.070 --> 00:56:32.850
is when I found a Protestant

00:56:32.850 --> 00:56:37.410
demonic possession that
involved levitation

00:56:37.410 --> 00:56:42.410
in Boston in 1693.

00:56:42.480 --> 00:56:45.100
A description of a young teenage girl

00:56:46.230 --> 00:56:48.750
who could not be held down

00:56:48.750 --> 00:56:53.340
or pulled down from the
ceiling by several burly men.

00:56:53.340 --> 00:56:56.130
And that's how they're described, burly,

00:56:56.130 --> 00:57:00.180
who had been brought there
by none other than the great

00:57:00.180 --> 00:57:02.763
Puritan minister Cotton Mather,

00:57:03.720 --> 00:57:07.540
who made them all sign
affidavits testifying

00:57:08.700 --> 00:57:12.453
they had been unable to restrain
the girl or pull her down.

00:57:13.920 --> 00:57:17.850
So there's symmetry, but there's asymmetry

00:57:17.850 --> 00:57:22.693
as far as the causality
of these levitations.

00:57:25.710 --> 00:57:30.150
But of course, levitation can be a trick.

00:57:30.150 --> 00:57:31.890
It's done frequently,

00:57:31.890 --> 00:57:34.383
and there, you can learn how to levitate.

00:57:38.070 --> 00:57:42.730
But my cases, if the
descriptions are in fact

00:57:44.820 --> 00:57:48.270
truthful testimony, there's
no way those levitations

00:57:48.270 --> 00:57:49.370
could have been faked.

00:57:50.730 --> 00:57:54.303
And it comes back in 19th and
20th century spiritualism.

00:57:57.660 --> 00:58:00.630
Upper left, a levitation

00:58:00.630 --> 00:58:05.070
by the great 19th century spiritualist,

00:58:05.070 --> 00:58:10.050
Daniel Dunglas Home, which took place

00:58:10.050 --> 00:58:14.253
in South Manchester,
Connecticut in the 1830s.

00:58:17.220 --> 00:58:21.680
And on the right you see a
Welsh spiritualist levitating.

00:58:24.060 --> 00:58:28.560
The reason everyone looks so asleep

00:58:28.560 --> 00:58:31.840
is that the photo is
taken in infrared light

00:58:32.730 --> 00:58:36.180
and the man holding the
switch for the camera

00:58:36.180 --> 00:58:38.913
is none other than the levitator.

00:58:42.000 --> 00:58:47.000
So levitation is absolutely possible.

00:58:48.870 --> 00:58:52.650
We have gadgets you can put
on your desk that levitate,

00:58:52.650 --> 00:58:55.053
we have electromagnetic trains.

00:58:56.010 --> 00:58:58.353
One of my sons spent two years in Japan.

00:58:59.220 --> 00:59:00.730
He levitated for two years

00:59:01.950 --> 00:59:04.443
every time he took the
electromagnetic train.

00:59:05.610 --> 00:59:09.077
And bilocation, we do it all the time.

00:59:16.320 --> 00:59:19.110
But of course, the
bilocations and levitations

00:59:19.110 --> 00:59:23.880
that I've studied are of a
very, very different sort.

00:59:23.880 --> 00:59:28.880
And yes, I will be constantly
reminded how ridiculous

00:59:32.490 --> 00:59:35.463
and how silly the subject is.

00:59:36.504 --> 00:59:38.154
But to me, it's not silly at all.

00:59:39.120 --> 00:59:39.953
And I'm not saying

00:59:39.953 --> 00:59:42.003
I can prove that any
of these things happen,

00:59:43.080 --> 00:59:44.850
what I'm saying is that

00:59:44.850 --> 00:59:47.230
historians need to adjust a little bit

00:59:48.120 --> 00:59:50.640
to supernatural events.

00:59:50.640 --> 00:59:54.933
Let's call them paranormal,
let's call them wild facts.

00:59:55.950 --> 00:59:58.840
Those brackets that I was taught to wield

00:59:59.880 --> 01:00:02.700
in this very building many years ago,

01:00:02.700 --> 01:00:06.333
don't you ever consider the
supernatural real, you can't.

01:00:07.530 --> 01:00:10.890
Those brackets need shrinking, let's say,

01:00:10.890 --> 01:00:15.180
and as I argue in my
book, if you want to be

01:00:15.180 --> 01:00:18.753
truly skeptical as any
good scientist should be,

01:00:20.400 --> 01:00:24.303
I could boast of being
Cartesian, following Descartes,

01:00:25.140 --> 01:00:27.393
De Omnibus et Dubitandum,

01:00:28.680 --> 01:00:31.380
everything ought to be doubted,

01:00:31.380 --> 01:00:35.073
including dogmatic materialism.

01:00:36.510 --> 01:00:41.510
And I'm eager to hear your
questions and comments.

01:00:43.320 --> 01:00:45.570
(applause)

01:00:52.080 --> 01:00:53.351
- Thank you so much.

01:00:53.351 --> 01:00:54.307
- You want both?

01:00:54.307 --> 01:00:56.894
- Yeah, I think that'll maybe ask.

01:00:56.894 --> 01:00:57.727
- Okay.

01:00:59.402 --> 01:01:01.069
- Yeah, switched on.

01:01:01.980 --> 01:01:05.313
- Okay, questions, thank you
so much, that was fascinating.

01:01:10.560 --> 01:01:12.630
- Just as a comment, I think a lot of

01:01:12.630 --> 01:01:16.560
controversy that you
faced could be prevented

01:01:16.560 --> 01:01:18.933
by changing the subtitle of your book.

01:01:20.010 --> 01:01:23.517
It could be, "What do we call Impossible?"

01:01:24.600 --> 01:01:26.880
because this is the crux of the matter.

01:01:26.880 --> 01:01:29.770
It's like, I bought my
son a book at some point

01:01:30.990 --> 01:01:33.727
by Nobel laureate and the title was

01:01:33.727 --> 01:01:35.847
"Impossible Takes Longer."

01:01:36.690 --> 01:01:41.220
And any field, I'm an experimental
biologist, in any field

01:01:41.220 --> 01:01:45.270
there small miracles where
half of the field says no,

01:01:45.270 --> 01:01:47.823
or all of them say no, it's impossible.

01:01:49.200 --> 01:01:52.830
They fire a person, he goes in hiding

01:01:52.830 --> 01:01:55.950
and then 25 years later or 50 years later,

01:01:55.950 --> 01:01:57.120
he gets a Nobel Prize

01:01:57.120 --> 01:02:00.840
because what was impossible is possible.

01:02:00.840 --> 01:02:04.350
The same thing if you gave
this lecture 200 years ago

01:02:04.350 --> 01:02:06.180
saying that you can talk to,

01:02:06.180 --> 01:02:08.400
I can talk to my son in France,

01:02:08.400 --> 01:02:11.910
it would be, so the key is
I think you an somebody,

01:02:11.910 --> 01:02:13.770
St. Augustine or Aquinas,

01:02:13.770 --> 01:02:17.880
you quoted it's impossible
is you call impossible

01:02:17.880 --> 01:02:22.271
what is unexplainable
to us at this stage of-

01:02:22.271 --> 01:02:23.104
- That's right.

01:02:23.104 --> 01:02:23.970
- What we know.
- That's right.

01:02:23.970 --> 01:02:27.480
- Because everything else
then becomes like literature.

01:02:27.480 --> 01:02:29.760
- Yeah, no, I agree with
you a hundred percent,

01:02:29.760 --> 01:02:34.760
but I have my tongue firmly
planted in my cheek by,

01:02:34.800 --> 01:02:36.550
I'm trying to rile up people

01:02:37.530 --> 01:02:40.080
by calling it a history of the impossible.

01:02:40.080 --> 01:02:43.230
Precisely because of
what you just say that,

01:02:43.230 --> 01:02:44.760
there are many things that one

01:02:44.760 --> 01:02:48.510
were once thought impossible that aren't,

01:02:48.510 --> 01:02:51.330
and actually I thought I
was doing something novel,

01:02:51.330 --> 01:02:53.620
but I have found several other books with

01:02:54.840 --> 01:02:59.840
the impossible in the title
used in a similar way,

01:02:59.940 --> 01:03:01.480
including this book by

01:03:02.520 --> 01:03:05.520
historian of religion at Rice
University, Jeffrey Krippal,

01:03:05.520 --> 01:03:10.520
K-R-I-P-P-A-L, authors of the Impossible,

01:03:11.220 --> 01:03:14.160
which is about 19th and
20th century writers

01:03:14.160 --> 01:03:16.690
from all kinds of specialties

01:03:17.700 --> 01:03:21.630
who write about impossible things.

01:03:21.630 --> 01:03:23.883
But yeah, I agree with you, it's.

01:03:25.343 --> 01:03:26.549
- Carlos.

01:03:26.549 --> 01:03:28.120
- Yeah, hi.

01:03:28.120 --> 01:03:31.380
- I'm Roberto Gonzalez Echevarría

01:03:31.380 --> 01:03:35.640
professor of literature
here at Yale now retired,

01:03:35.640 --> 01:03:39.488
but I'm here also as a member
of the Yale Aviation Club

01:03:39.488 --> 01:03:41.670
(laughter)

01:03:41.670 --> 01:03:46.670
because I'm a pilot, as is Taia here,

01:03:46.920 --> 01:03:50.070
who's also a member of
the Yale Aviation Club.

01:03:50.070 --> 01:03:53.820
We laugh because isn't
there something humorous

01:03:53.820 --> 01:03:56.193
in this business of flying nuns?

01:03:57.090 --> 01:03:59.307
- Yes, yeah, well, certainly there is.

01:03:59.307 --> 01:04:01.840
And you know, most of the flying nuns

01:04:06.690 --> 01:04:10.050
end up being processed by the inquisition

01:04:10.050 --> 01:04:11.913
and discovered to be frauds.

01:04:12.900 --> 01:04:17.020
So the issue is, why are some

01:04:18.030 --> 01:04:22.080
deemed legitimate and some not legitimate?

01:04:22.080 --> 01:04:23.730
Why are there tricks discovered

01:04:23.730 --> 01:04:27.480
and why are other people's
tricks not discovered?

01:04:27.480 --> 01:04:31.260
We actually had a very
interesting discussion in my,

01:04:31.260 --> 01:04:33.060
one of my classes yesterday.

01:04:33.060 --> 01:04:36.210
It's of course history
of the supernatural.

01:04:36.210 --> 01:04:39.330
And we were dealing with
19th century spiritualists,

01:04:39.330 --> 01:04:41.370
seances and so on.

01:04:41.370 --> 01:04:42.690
- [Priyamvada] Mesmerism, right?

01:04:42.690 --> 01:04:45.510
- And mesmerism and theosophy.

01:04:45.510 --> 01:04:49.173
But anyway, the image you
saw of Daniel Dunglas Home,

01:04:50.640 --> 01:04:55.350
he is the one spiritualist
medium that no scientist

01:04:55.350 --> 01:05:00.000
could crack, discover or prove

01:05:00.000 --> 01:05:03.660
that he had some trickery
involved in his things.

01:05:03.660 --> 01:05:06.603
So that's what keeps me flying.

01:05:08.580 --> 01:05:11.140
But St. Joseph is your patron saint

01:05:12.180 --> 01:05:16.470
or anyone who flies, it's the
patron saint of all flyers,

01:05:16.470 --> 01:05:21.470
ironically, a job given to him by

01:05:21.690 --> 01:05:23.460
American bomber pilots

01:05:23.460 --> 01:05:26.040
who were bombing the hell out of Italy

01:05:26.040 --> 01:05:28.590
during the Second World War, who proposed,

01:05:28.590 --> 01:05:31.293
hey, here's a good patron
saint for all flyers.

01:05:33.600 --> 01:05:36.363
- If I may follow up on
the earlier question,

01:05:37.920 --> 01:05:40.770
it is true that all
knowledge is provisional

01:05:40.770 --> 01:05:45.770
and that what we know even
in science is best to date

01:05:45.840 --> 01:05:49.383
and is apt to get
refined, changed, altered.

01:05:50.490 --> 01:05:55.490
But do you think that
when these visualizations

01:05:57.690 --> 01:05:59.670
and these claims were made,

01:05:59.670 --> 01:06:03.030
people were not really
looking for an explanation?

01:06:03.030 --> 01:06:04.950
Although I noticed that you noted that

01:06:04.950 --> 01:06:07.500
any time you had someone levitating,

01:06:07.500 --> 01:06:09.210
there was a witness,

01:06:09.210 --> 01:06:11.610
there was another person
in the frame who could

01:06:11.610 --> 01:06:13.053
testify that this.

01:06:14.130 --> 01:06:18.690
So there's sort of this nod to evidence,

01:06:18.690 --> 01:06:22.830
but at the same time, when
you look at those images,

01:06:22.830 --> 01:06:26.400
at least at the time, they were
not meant to be challenged.

01:06:26.400 --> 01:06:28.020
- That's right, no,
they're not meant to be.

01:06:28.020 --> 01:06:30.237
- No, cause was sought.

01:06:30.237 --> 01:06:31.380
- No, and as a matter of fact,

01:06:31.380 --> 01:06:34.560
the Protestants too are
looking for causality,

01:06:34.560 --> 01:06:37.110
there's always a search for causality.

01:06:37.110 --> 01:06:41.250
But why does Cotton Mather
insist that these burly men

01:06:41.250 --> 01:06:43.110
sign an affidavit?

01:06:43.110 --> 01:06:46.500
Say, yes, I was there and
I tried to hold her down.

01:06:46.500 --> 01:06:49.350
It's because they know
there are challenges.

01:06:49.350 --> 01:06:51.930
And I think that many of those paintings

01:06:51.930 --> 01:06:53.070
actually were commissioned

01:06:53.070 --> 01:06:56.920
because those commissioning them knew

01:06:57.900 --> 01:07:00.970
that this was not believed
by a hundred percent of

01:07:02.400 --> 01:07:05.703
the people anywhere, that
there was skepticism.

01:07:08.010 --> 01:07:10.800
And I found not in regards to levitation,

01:07:10.800 --> 01:07:14.070
but another miracle
attributed to St. Teresa,

01:07:14.070 --> 01:07:17.133
her corpse refused to decompose.

01:07:18.780 --> 01:07:22.650
And in one of her hagiographies,
the hagiographer says,

01:07:22.650 --> 01:07:26.730
you don't wanna believe,
go ahead, don't believe.

01:07:26.730 --> 01:07:29.280
But if you don't believe
this, you shouldn't believe

01:07:29.280 --> 01:07:31.650
any of the miracles in the Bible either.

01:07:31.650 --> 01:07:36.650
Which is a, I don't know
if that's a good argument.

01:07:40.140 --> 01:07:41.240
- Any other questions?

01:07:44.730 --> 01:07:46.192
- If I can add to,

01:07:46.192 --> 01:07:50.160
and again, my background
is experimental biology.

01:07:50.160 --> 01:07:53.220
It was absolutely, I would call it,

01:07:53.220 --> 01:07:56.880
materialist like in the
bad meaning of this word.

01:07:56.880 --> 01:08:01.880
And then my son sent me an
article from a paper from Lancet

01:08:02.070 --> 01:08:06.480
and Lancet, it's bonafide medical journal.

01:08:06.480 --> 01:08:10.440
And it described the
case of a person who was

01:08:10.440 --> 01:08:12.540
hit by a truck or something like this,

01:08:12.540 --> 01:08:15.660
brought in the emergency room, intubated.

01:08:15.660 --> 01:08:20.660
So he was what they call flatlined, right?

01:08:20.760 --> 01:08:25.230
And then after that, he
was rescued obviously,

01:08:25.230 --> 01:08:30.230
and he was in his room

01:08:30.570 --> 01:08:33.390
and when the nurse came, he
said, oh, this is, she is,

01:08:33.390 --> 01:08:37.080
she took my dentures so he could describe

01:08:37.080 --> 01:08:38.310
that she took the dentures

01:08:38.310 --> 01:08:41.190
and put in a certain drawer, right?

01:08:41.190 --> 01:08:43.140
While he was flatlined,

01:08:43.140 --> 01:08:46.170
and the paper presented
as one of many cases

01:08:46.170 --> 01:08:47.880
and discusses others

01:08:47.880 --> 01:08:49.620
and as an experimental biologist,

01:08:49.620 --> 01:08:53.550
I had to take it as a
fact, from what you say,

01:08:53.550 --> 01:08:54.600
wild fact, not fact,

01:08:54.600 --> 01:08:56.790
I think it should be
distinction between facts,

01:08:56.790 --> 01:09:00.840
which is something verifiable,
and opinions, right?

01:09:00.840 --> 01:09:02.280
Somebody can have an opinion

01:09:02.280 --> 01:09:04.170
and somebody is flying, you know?

01:09:04.170 --> 01:09:05.790
But a fact is a fact.

01:09:05.790 --> 01:09:08.640
So the thing is, and it turns
out there's a department

01:09:08.640 --> 01:09:12.540
called Perceptual Studies in
the University of Virginia,

01:09:12.540 --> 01:09:17.540
that like follow the
work of William James.

01:09:18.450 --> 01:09:21.060
And multiple things are documented,

01:09:21.060 --> 01:09:23.520
obviously there was a lot
of fraudsters everywhere,

01:09:23.520 --> 01:09:25.950
but in science as we know it and accept,

01:09:25.950 --> 01:09:27.630
also very fraudsters.

01:09:27.630 --> 01:09:31.320
But this is something that is verifiable

01:09:31.320 --> 01:09:33.150
and unexplainable.

01:09:33.150 --> 01:09:36.510
It's a modern miracle
you can call it, right?

01:09:36.510 --> 01:09:40.320
But it's undeniable
because it's documented,

01:09:40.320 --> 01:09:42.120
published in Lancet and everything.

01:09:42.120 --> 01:09:43.680
So maybe this is what I,

01:09:43.680 --> 01:09:45.330
I'm looking forward to read your book,

01:09:45.330 --> 01:09:46.590
maybe I'm just repeating what's-

01:09:46.590 --> 01:09:48.750
- Thanks, well, I'm glad
you brought up this parallel

01:09:48.750 --> 01:09:53.580
because these impossible
things are on a spectrum,

01:09:53.580 --> 01:09:55.290
I like to think.

01:09:55.290 --> 01:09:57.690
And near death experiences, which is what,

01:09:57.690 --> 01:10:01.390
the subject category under
which this research falls

01:10:02.940 --> 01:10:05.070
is part of the spectrum

01:10:05.070 --> 01:10:08.130
because there's something
undeniable about it.

01:10:08.130 --> 01:10:11.350
But there's a vehement disagreement among

01:10:12.840 --> 01:10:17.840
scientists and medical personnel
who study this phenomenon.

01:10:18.330 --> 01:10:19.890
And I just spent the past three days

01:10:19.890 --> 01:10:21.723
reading some of this back and forth,

01:10:23.070 --> 01:10:27.060
and I was stunned to
discover that there is a

01:10:27.060 --> 01:10:32.060
journal of near death
experiences, which was run out of

01:10:32.580 --> 01:10:35.970
University of Virginia in the center for,

01:10:35.970 --> 01:10:38.405
what was the title of the?

01:10:38.405 --> 01:10:39.750
(audience member answers question)

01:10:39.750 --> 01:10:41.568
Yeah, perceptual studies.

01:10:41.568 --> 01:10:42.840
And ironically,

01:10:42.840 --> 01:10:45.480
I spent 15 years at the
University of Virginia

01:10:45.480 --> 01:10:47.553
and didn't know this center was there.

01:10:48.930 --> 01:10:52.350
I had to come here 27
years later, find out,

01:10:52.350 --> 01:10:54.663
oh, they were next door.

01:10:55.596 --> 01:10:58.740
But yeah, I appreciate what
you're saying very much.

01:10:58.740 --> 01:10:59.710
- But I guess the question is,

01:10:59.710 --> 01:11:02.260
there are many such things in
neuroscience as well, right?

01:11:02.260 --> 01:11:06.480
There are phantom arms, people
feel pain in phantom arms

01:11:06.480 --> 01:11:08.610
and so on, and the question is,

01:11:08.610 --> 01:11:10.950
I'm not sure miracle is the word,

01:11:10.950 --> 01:11:13.320
I think mystery might be a better word

01:11:13.320 --> 01:11:16.342
for something like that,
because miracle is loaded.

01:11:16.342 --> 01:11:17.175
- You feel something,

01:11:17.175 --> 01:11:19.560
before about the near death experiences,

01:11:19.560 --> 01:11:21.750
I thought, well, if you're drunk,

01:11:21.750 --> 01:11:24.180
I mean, I didn't experience
myself to this extent,

01:11:24.180 --> 01:11:29.140
but I only see a small
green animals running around

01:11:30.167 --> 01:11:33.630
and people can see many things.

01:11:33.630 --> 01:11:37.320
But if you can verify what the person-

01:11:37.320 --> 01:11:39.390
- And replicate and replicate.

01:11:39.390 --> 01:11:43.890
- Verify, yeah, just say, it
says it was there and it is,

01:11:43.890 --> 01:11:45.663
and there's no way he could see.

01:11:47.580 --> 01:11:52.119
You have to stop and say,
look, we dunno how we work,

01:11:52.119 --> 01:11:55.625
we dunno how the brain
works, something fundamental.

01:11:55.625 --> 01:11:58.627
And like this stuff comes out in there.

01:11:58.627 --> 01:12:01.360
- There are numerous puzzling

01:12:04.710 --> 01:12:09.710
wild facts of this sort that
go into the 20th century.

01:12:11.610 --> 01:12:14.490
You might re recall, I refused to call

01:12:14.490 --> 01:12:18.540
Maria de Agreda the greatest
bilocator of all time.

01:12:18.540 --> 01:12:21.240
Reason for this is that just
at the end of my research,

01:12:21.240 --> 01:12:24.003
it was too late for me to
include this in the book,

01:12:24.930 --> 01:12:27.090
and it was the wrong century.

01:12:27.090 --> 01:12:29.760
I discovered that in the 20th century,

01:12:29.760 --> 01:12:33.840
there was a French nun who
bilocated more frequently

01:12:33.840 --> 01:12:38.840
and even more outrageously
than Maria De Agreda.

01:12:39.433 --> 01:12:41.553
And at the very same time,

01:12:43.020 --> 01:12:48.020
rescued French resistance
fighters and downed allied pilots

01:12:48.960 --> 01:12:51.630
by dressing them as nuns

01:12:51.630 --> 01:12:55.200
and escorting them out of
the hands of the Nazis.

01:12:55.200 --> 01:12:57.280
But most of her levitations

01:13:00.270 --> 01:13:05.270
were to prisons and concentration camps.

01:13:05.280 --> 01:13:08.400
And some of the testimonies
are from individuals

01:13:08.400 --> 01:13:11.820
who claim she came, comforted them,

01:13:11.820 --> 01:13:15.093
or in some cases helped them escape.

01:13:16.830 --> 01:13:20.520
And for some odd, I can't figure this out

01:13:20.520 --> 01:13:23.160
because she died in 1951,

01:13:23.160 --> 01:13:27.033
and there's a lot of French
language literature on her.

01:13:28.290 --> 01:13:31.410
There's nothing in
English, nothing in German,

01:13:31.410 --> 01:13:32.340
nothing in Spanish,

01:13:32.340 --> 01:13:34.683
nothing in any language other than French.

01:13:35.580 --> 01:13:38.613
She's not known outside of France.

01:13:40.020 --> 01:13:42.360
You can find her on YouTube, however.

01:13:42.360 --> 01:13:44.733
But, that's always a dicey proposition.

01:13:46.170 --> 01:13:48.390
But yeah, there are these puzzling things.

01:13:48.390 --> 01:13:52.473
I also found a very, somebody
again emailed me this,

01:13:53.520 --> 01:13:56.190
something that happened during the 1970s

01:13:56.190 --> 01:14:00.790
that I was totally unaware of, which was

01:14:02.790 --> 01:14:07.790
an apparition of the Virgin
Mary in the 1970s of all places,

01:14:08.460 --> 01:14:12.693
a suburb of Cairo, Zaytoun,

01:14:14.490 --> 01:14:19.200
which was viewed by over a million people

01:14:19.200 --> 01:14:21.330
over a period of two years every night

01:14:21.330 --> 01:14:25.530
and was in all the Egyptian
newspapers, of course,

01:14:25.530 --> 01:14:29.793
but never made it to any
newspaper I read in the 1970s.

01:14:30.960 --> 01:14:35.960
And most of the viewers were
Muslims, not Christians.

01:14:36.360 --> 01:14:40.590
So these, I love these wild
facts that just throw you

01:14:40.590 --> 01:14:44.733
for a loop and leave you
wondering how it is that,

01:14:47.400 --> 01:14:49.230
well, one way of put it

01:14:49.230 --> 01:14:52.900
with small brackets, how
belief is constructed

01:14:54.780 --> 01:14:59.553
or how belief is reported.

01:15:02.100 --> 01:15:06.840
Those two things have a lot
to do with people's exposure

01:15:06.840 --> 01:15:08.313
to these kinds of phenomena.

01:15:15.600 --> 01:15:19.290
- Thank you so much for your
presentation, it was wonderful.

01:15:19.290 --> 01:15:22.140
You talked about how people are reporting,

01:15:22.140 --> 01:15:25.020
the people who wrote these
stories 500 years ago,

01:15:25.020 --> 01:15:28.140
or even our sacred stories
had a whole different vision

01:15:28.140 --> 01:15:31.650
and understanding of the
cosmos than we do today.

01:15:31.650 --> 01:15:35.760
And yet this is particularly
true of our theologians,

01:15:35.760 --> 01:15:37.410
we still go back and interpreted them

01:15:37.410 --> 01:15:40.320
based on their
interpretation of the cosmos.

01:15:40.320 --> 01:15:42.360
They wrote it from that, and we look at it

01:15:42.360 --> 01:15:44.520
and say, well, this is how it was,

01:15:44.520 --> 01:15:47.113
even though they had this
whole different vision of it.

01:15:47.113 --> 01:15:50.550
I also just wanted to say
your story about Mary,

01:15:50.550 --> 01:15:54.060
Mary is one of the most
revered women in Islam,

01:15:54.060 --> 01:15:57.240
she's one of the only women
mentioned in the Quran.

01:15:57.240 --> 01:16:00.180
So it's not surprising
at all that in Egypt

01:16:00.180 --> 01:16:02.670
it would be a lot of Muslim
women that were there.

01:16:02.670 --> 01:16:05.220
It's not, people sometimes
are shocked by that,

01:16:05.220 --> 01:16:06.870
but it's not a surprise.

01:16:06.870 --> 01:16:11.430
But the whole idea of how do we reimagine

01:16:11.430 --> 01:16:13.980
our science and our theology

01:16:13.980 --> 01:16:16.380
not based on what they wrote 2000 years,

01:16:16.380 --> 01:16:19.380
and, not to throw out the
baby with the bath water

01:16:19.380 --> 01:16:21.270
to use an old Irish expression,

01:16:21.270 --> 01:16:23.760
to take that wonderful work,

01:16:23.760 --> 01:16:27.720
but understand it from how we
understand the cosmos today?

01:16:27.720 --> 01:16:30.540
- Right, and no, I agree
with you that that is a,

01:16:30.540 --> 01:16:32.430
that's a real challenge.

01:16:32.430 --> 01:16:37.430
However, the testimony
is not all from clerics

01:16:37.440 --> 01:16:40.110
who are writing hagiographies,

01:16:40.110 --> 01:16:43.203
the canonization inquests are,

01:16:44.760 --> 01:16:46.980
they're almost like legal proceedings.

01:16:46.980 --> 01:16:50.130
And they ask for testimony

01:16:50.130 --> 01:16:51.750
from all different sorts of people.

01:16:51.750 --> 01:16:53.100
- [Moderator] They're rooted in faith.

01:16:53.100 --> 01:16:54.438
- I'm sorry?

01:16:54.438 --> 01:16:56.640
- [Priyamvada] All of these,

01:16:56.640 --> 01:16:59.940
interlocutors in that are rooted in faith.

01:16:59.940 --> 01:17:04.750
- Right, and this scholar at
Rice University, I mentioned

01:17:06.420 --> 01:17:10.630
Jeffrey Krippal, he has argued that

01:17:12.870 --> 01:17:16.980
belief as a social phenomenon,

01:17:16.980 --> 01:17:20.010
as a social fact has a lot to do

01:17:20.010 --> 01:17:24.450
with the occurrence of
these impossible events

01:17:24.450 --> 01:17:26.310
and impossible phenomena.

01:17:26.310 --> 01:17:30.480
That there's a correlation
between the belief

01:17:30.480 --> 01:17:32.730
and the events.

01:17:32.730 --> 01:17:37.730
Although there's no easy way
to explain it scientifically

01:17:37.890 --> 01:17:41.700
unless one has discovered proof of,

01:17:41.700 --> 01:17:44.430
interpenetrating dimensions,

01:17:44.430 --> 01:17:48.420
which has yet to yet to be accomplished.

01:17:48.420 --> 01:17:51.720
But I think my Welsh scientist

01:17:51.720 --> 01:17:56.720
was very, very excited by
the suspension of time space,

01:17:57.570 --> 01:17:59.430
something I cannot understand.

01:17:59.430 --> 01:18:02.310
And I don't know what, how widely believed

01:18:02.310 --> 01:18:04.893
he will be in his own
scientific community.

01:18:08.550 --> 01:18:11.373
- We close with, oh, one final question.

01:18:17.050 --> 01:18:21.150
- Some of the narrative
details of the depictions,

01:18:21.150 --> 01:18:25.470
for instance, the crowd
is pretty reverential.

01:18:25.470 --> 01:18:26.303
- Oh yeah.

01:18:26.303 --> 01:18:28.020
- [Audience member]
Well, and I'm thinking if

01:18:28.020 --> 01:18:31.050
someone levitated in front of me,

01:18:31.050 --> 01:18:32.250
if I didn't know them,

01:18:32.250 --> 01:18:35.340
I might be scared that I
was gonna levitate too,

01:18:35.340 --> 01:18:38.280
or that they were gonna fall on me.

01:18:38.280 --> 01:18:40.830
If it was my friend
Nancy, I would be pulling,

01:18:40.830 --> 01:18:44.430
holding onto her so that
she didn't float away.

01:18:44.430 --> 01:18:46.860
But there seems like a
standard in these photos,

01:18:46.860 --> 01:18:51.860
the pictures, that this is
how you treat a levitation.

01:18:52.080 --> 01:18:53.790
- That's the depiction,

01:18:53.790 --> 01:18:56.013
the actual accounts are very different.

01:18:57.660 --> 01:19:01.200
People are frightened half to death often,

01:19:01.200 --> 01:19:02.820
in the description of the accounts,

01:19:02.820 --> 01:19:05.220
they're frightened half to death,

01:19:05.220 --> 01:19:09.963
and terror is one of the words used.

01:19:11.040 --> 01:19:15.093
Or the equivalent of
the English word terror.

01:19:16.200 --> 01:19:21.200
The funny thing is that, I
don't know if you know of the

01:19:21.330 --> 01:19:23.133
illusionist David Blaine.

01:19:24.450 --> 01:19:27.940
David Blaine, there was a
documentary on David Blaine

01:19:28.890 --> 01:19:31.560
years ago, like at least two decades ago,

01:19:31.560 --> 01:19:34.593
where he was performing tricks
on the street in Manhattan.

01:19:35.970 --> 01:19:37.953
And one of his tricks was levitation,

01:19:40.530 --> 01:19:42.980
which has all to do with
how you place your feet.

01:19:44.490 --> 01:19:48.660
And the camera in this documentary

01:19:48.660 --> 01:19:53.580
was always aimed at his feet,
very rarely at his whole body.

01:19:53.580 --> 01:19:57.780
But you could see that
from certain perspective,

01:19:57.780 --> 01:20:00.450
it looked like his feet
were off the ground.

01:20:00.450 --> 01:20:01.950
But what I liked is that the camera

01:20:01.950 --> 01:20:04.023
also turned to the audience,

01:20:05.070 --> 01:20:09.330
and at least the clips they
chose for the documentary,

01:20:09.330 --> 01:20:13.020
everyone is backing away from him.

01:20:13.020 --> 01:20:16.050
The body language is
constant in all those clips,

01:20:16.050 --> 01:20:20.380
everybody's going and then
they, there's an image

01:20:21.480 --> 01:20:26.480
of a young woman saying,
oh, he must be very holy.

01:20:30.330 --> 01:20:34.260
So yeah, if I saw somebody levitating,

01:20:34.260 --> 01:20:36.753
I think I would maybe run out of the room.

01:20:38.130 --> 01:20:43.130
Not just back away, but,
scary stuff sometimes.

01:20:44.940 --> 01:20:46.020
I did fail to mention

01:20:46.020 --> 01:20:48.603
that every time Joseph
of Cupertino went up,

01:20:49.890 --> 01:20:53.223
he let out some sound, loud sound.

01:20:54.120 --> 01:20:59.120
And the Italian words are shout,

01:21:01.320 --> 01:21:06.320
strilo, or screech and urlo,

01:21:06.510 --> 01:21:08.800
some kind of basso profundo

01:21:11.250 --> 01:21:14.463
detonation of sorts, which one time,

01:21:16.050 --> 01:21:18.060
according to several testimonies,

01:21:18.060 --> 01:21:21.840
made the walls of the monastery shake

01:21:21.840 --> 01:21:25.320
and the monks run out
of their cells yelling,

01:21:25.320 --> 01:21:29.133
earthquake, earthquake, that's scary too.

01:21:31.350 --> 01:21:32.970
Did it happen?

01:21:32.970 --> 01:21:36.090
Well, nobody will be able to prove it,

01:21:36.090 --> 01:21:37.203
we don't have film.

01:21:38.700 --> 01:21:42.790
but I just received an email
yesterday from some TV program

01:21:44.844 --> 01:21:48.820
that claims they have
film of people levitating

01:21:49.920 --> 01:21:53.280
and they want me to comment and I said,

01:21:53.280 --> 01:21:57.930
I don't know of any films
of people levitating,

01:21:57.930 --> 01:22:00.153
sure sign me up, I wanna see these.

01:22:04.110 --> 01:22:08.220
- Carlos, this wonderful
presentation reminded me

01:22:08.220 --> 01:22:13.080
of the miracle of Lanciano and
other eucharistic miracles.

01:22:13.080 --> 01:22:16.440
So the heart of every Catholic mass

01:22:16.440 --> 01:22:18.690
is the consecration as you mentioned.

01:22:18.690 --> 01:22:21.660
So it's, suppose you could term it a

01:22:21.660 --> 01:22:23.430
quotidian kind of miracle, right?

01:22:23.430 --> 01:22:27.480
It's oft repeated every
mass, every day, every hour,

01:22:27.480 --> 01:22:28.680
everywhere in the world.

01:22:29.670 --> 01:22:32.250
There are like the miracle of Lanciano,

01:22:32.250 --> 01:22:37.250
explicitly rendered
hosts eucharists, right?

01:22:38.070 --> 01:22:41.520
That bleed and are still preserved thusly.

01:22:41.520 --> 01:22:43.320
You mentioned Luther

01:22:43.320 --> 01:22:46.710
and how he believed in the true presence,

01:22:46.710 --> 01:22:48.270
but subsequent Protestants

01:22:48.270 --> 01:22:51.810
and obviously Protestantism
in general strayed from that.

01:22:51.810 --> 01:22:52.950
Could you walk us through a bit

01:22:52.950 --> 01:22:56.280
how we went from Luther
believing in the presence

01:22:56.280 --> 01:22:59.760
to his contemporaries, let's
say, and soon thereafter?

01:22:59.760 --> 01:23:02.190
- Yeah, sure, it's at the
critical turning point

01:23:02.190 --> 01:23:04.390
in the history of the
Protestant Reformation

01:23:05.460 --> 01:23:09.960
is that Luther continued to
believe in the real presence,

01:23:09.960 --> 01:23:14.250
but he didn't like the
term transubstantiation

01:23:14.250 --> 01:23:16.770
or the concept of transubstantiation

01:23:16.770 --> 01:23:19.270
'cause it required you
to be an Aristotelian

01:23:20.430 --> 01:23:25.430
and understand what was meant
by substance and accident.

01:23:26.070 --> 01:23:28.530
And also because there's no description

01:23:28.530 --> 01:23:31.590
of what happens in Jesus's word.

01:23:31.590 --> 01:23:34.110
So he was being very biblical about it.

01:23:34.110 --> 01:23:35.910
But he insisted if Jesus says,

01:23:35.910 --> 01:23:39.963
take this and eat, this is my body, right?

01:23:41.100 --> 01:23:43.440
That was the bottom line for him.

01:23:43.440 --> 01:23:47.640
So in 1529, he and all
the other major reformers

01:23:47.640 --> 01:23:52.560
of that period meet in one
room at Marburg in Germany.

01:23:52.560 --> 01:23:57.120
They had been brought
together by Philip of Hesse,

01:23:57.120 --> 01:24:01.230
the most powerful Lutheran
prince of the time,

01:24:01.230 --> 01:24:04.110
so that they can form a military alliance

01:24:04.110 --> 01:24:07.680
among all the different
branches of Protestantism,

01:24:07.680 --> 01:24:11.403
including those Swiss who fight so well.

01:24:13.230 --> 01:24:15.750
No agree, they agree on 13 points,

01:24:15.750 --> 01:24:18.903
they cannot agree on 14, the Eucharist.

01:24:19.800 --> 01:24:23.200
And Luther actually writes on
the table according, we have

01:24:24.540 --> 01:24:28.560
notes that were taken by
four different people,

01:24:28.560 --> 01:24:30.210
so we have four different transcripts.

01:24:30.210 --> 01:24:34.180
They all say, Luther wrote
on the table with chalk

01:24:35.640 --> 01:24:40.640
Hoc est corpus meum in
Latin, this is my body.

01:24:41.970 --> 01:24:44.670
And then he put a little cloth over it.

01:24:44.670 --> 01:24:46.800
And every time, the Swiss, especially

01:24:46.800 --> 01:24:50.040
who didn't believe in the
real presence brought up.

01:24:50.040 --> 01:24:53.790
But is, could be a metaphor.

01:24:53.790 --> 01:24:57.153
Jesus also said, I am the
vine, you are the branches.

01:25:01.380 --> 01:25:02.580
He's being metaphorical.

01:25:02.580 --> 01:25:04.350
Luther would say, no,
no, it's not a metaphor.

01:25:04.350 --> 01:25:07.260
No, no, no, and then he'd
lift up the little cloth

01:25:07.260 --> 01:25:12.050
and point to the word
est, est, est means est.

01:25:12.050 --> 01:25:13.980
It kind of reminded me of you know,

01:25:13.980 --> 01:25:17.430
the Bill Clinton affair years ago,

01:25:17.430 --> 01:25:19.620
what is something?

01:25:19.620 --> 01:25:23.160
The semantics got involved,
but Luther finally got just,

01:25:23.160 --> 01:25:26.490
they started insulting
each other at Marburg

01:25:26.490 --> 01:25:28.050
and Luther at one point says,

01:25:28.050 --> 01:25:29.850
and you'll have to pardon my German,

01:25:31.920 --> 01:25:35.880
look, Christ not only
said this is my body,

01:25:35.880 --> 01:25:38.283
he says, take this and eat.

01:25:39.720 --> 01:25:43.743
He says, if God had
commanded me to eat scheiss,

01:25:45.120 --> 01:25:46.023
I would have to.

01:25:47.370 --> 01:25:50.790
So it's the word of God,
God can do anything.

01:25:50.790 --> 01:25:52.420
So the Swiss come back at him

01:25:53.280 --> 01:25:58.053
and say, look, Jesus has a human body.

01:25:59.250 --> 01:26:01.360
His resurrected body was fully human

01:26:02.250 --> 01:26:03.500
and it went up to heaven.

01:26:04.530 --> 01:26:07.593
A human body cannot be
in two places at once.

01:26:08.760 --> 01:26:11.313
Luther responds, you're
being too mathematical.

01:26:13.380 --> 01:26:18.000
God is all powerful, God can
be everywhere if he wants to.

01:26:18.000 --> 01:26:20.550
And the discussion goes
on and on and on and on.

01:26:20.550 --> 01:26:24.633
They can't agree and no military alliance.

01:26:25.830 --> 01:26:30.830
So that's how it diverged.

01:26:32.430 --> 01:26:36.090
And in the Anglican Church,
the definition of the Eucharist

01:26:36.090 --> 01:26:41.090
is unclear enough to suggest
both symbol and presence,

01:26:42.240 --> 01:26:45.903
at least the 1549 book of common prayer.

01:26:46.860 --> 01:26:48.663
So there are these differences.

01:26:54.150 --> 01:26:55.770
- Thank you so much once again.

01:26:55.770 --> 01:26:57.326
So let's all thank Carlos for-

01:26:57.326 --> 01:27:00.328
- Thank you, thank you all, thank you.

01:27:00.328 --> 01:27:02.578
(applause)

01:27:03.964 --> 01:27:06.381
(slow music)

